window.harnessChunks["rows-199bcac457a0c36e.js"]=["<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T19:15:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aaif-goose/goose\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Goose</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aaif-goose/goose/tree/50666ae0b9a51e260b52b7efbab2e4e020346e94\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 50666ae0b9a51e260b52b7efbab2e4e020346e94\">50666ae &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the standard runtime autonomously selects and executes actions/tools within configured bounds and iterates from results.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: no first-party S2 witness was established. The orchestrator permits top-level cross-session messages and permits delegated subagents to message siblings with the same parent, but generic communication does not identify a specific inter-S1 interference, conflict or oscillation together with a relation that attenuates it and feeds the result back into later S1 behavior. Subagent/fork support remains delegation rather than sufficient S2 evidence.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the first-party orchestrator mode gives the autonomous top-level Goose agent a whole-system current view across sessions (listsessions / viewsession) and actual intervention authority over current commitments and operational capacity (startagent, sendmessage, interruptagent). These tools are directly model-visible and their effects are closed by the runtime; once the supported mode is enabled, a developer does not need to compose a separate autonomous actor, S3 decision authority, or closure loop. defaultenabled: false and hidden: true do not convert an otherwise closed agent-owned mode into C. The decisive evidence was already present at the accepted review ref, making this a same-ref correction rather than a new-ref architectural change.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: no alternative, sufficiently independent complementary audit path with materially different access to operational reality was established. Session viewing is part of the same S3 current-control surface, not a distinct audit relation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: context management, retries and task reasoning concern current execution; no distinct externally and prospectively oriented intelligence loop that develops adaptation options and returns them into current capability was found.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: system prompts, permissions, extensions and runtime configuration remain developer/user supplied rather than an autonomous legitimate identity/ultimate-policy decision path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c18ecd44bdd2a63ce0893a114d25f5d3cc75a76a/assessments/goose.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c18ecd44bdd2a63ce0893a114d25f5d3cc75a76a\">c18ecd4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T18:08:39+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Onelevenvy/flock\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Flock</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Onelevenvy/flock/tree/2eac827aa803ae2ee8578e401a864e116cd994d3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 2eac827aa803ae2ee8578e401a864e116cd994d3\">2eac827 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously perform environment-facing task work through an iterative model/tool loop and return resulting observations into subsequent action selection. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, changing local/project state, model responses, tool outputs/errors, sandbox/browser/process state and other execution observations requiring context-sensitive next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select the next tool/action from current messages and tool results, then decide whether another operational action is needed or the task/run can terminate. - Decision owner: the active first-party Flock agent loop, including agent nodes used inside declarative workflows. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: AgentEngine::run, Flock&#39;s modelrequest/calltools graph, tool registry/executor, sandbox creation, checkpoint state, retries/timeouts, iteration limits and workflow state transport. - Closure path: task/messages/environment state → agent model selects a tool/action → first-party Flock tool/runtime executes it → tool result or changed environment returns into agent messages/state → agent chooses the next action or terminates. - Boundary reachability: this is the core shipped flock-agent path used by ordinary runs; workflow agent nodes expose the same class of bounded autonomous tool loop in the supported declarative workflow surface. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic graph/tool machinery transports and enforces execution, but it does not choose the context-sensitive next operational action; removing the model-driven agent leaves no owner of that S1 discretion. - Evidence: crates/flock-agent/src/engine/run/run.rs, crates/flock-agent/src/graph/builder.rs, crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/agent.rs. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 coordination path is established at the reviewed recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established; workflows can contain multiple operational agent nodes, but the reviewed shipped mechanisms do not identify a specific actual or structurally evidenced interference, conflict or oscillation among those S1 units that a coordination relation is designed to attenuate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no S2-specific actor is shown choosing or revising a coordination response to cross-S1 disturbance. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: graph edges, conditional routing, fan-out to multiple successors, shared workflow state, checkpointing, retries/timeouts and scheduler execution. - Closure path: these mechanisms move and sequence state/work among nodes, but no interference → attenuation decision → changed subsequent S1 behavior closure is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: individual agent nodes own their local operational choices; graph topology and scheduler semantics are deterministic/user-authored. Neither fact establishes autonomous coordination of a concrete inter-S1 disturbance. - Evidence: crates/flock-workflow/src/builder.rs, crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/agent.rs, crates/flock-agent/src/graph/mod.rs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no whole-system current-control function is established in the reviewed standard distribution. - Disturbance / variety regulated: workflow progress, node failures, retries, timeouts and branch routing are regulated mechanically, but no first-party actor is shown regulating current shared resources, commitments, priorities or constraints on behalf of the whole Flock organization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no shipped actor has both a whole-system current view and discretionary authority to revise relevant organization-wide commitments/resources/priorities/interventions. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow scheduler/compiled graph, checkpoints, retries/timeouts, cancellation state, workflow event sink, cron/headless execution controls and per-session approval state. - Closure path: these mechanisms close execution and error-handling transitions according to configured topology/policy, not a discretionary whole-system S3 control conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: agent nodes select local task actions, while the workflow compiler/scheduler enforces configured structure. Neither worker selection nor deterministic current-state execution supplies the broader S3 decision right. - Evidence: crates/flock-workflow/src/builder.rs, crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/common.rs, crates/flock-agent/src/engine/run/run.rs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material complementary operational-audit path is established for the current S1/S3 control boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Flock exposes tool results, workflow events, retries and evaluation utilities, but no reviewed supported path independently challenges an operational unit&#39;s ordinary current-control claim and returns that finding into S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S3 at the current-control layer. - Decision owner: none established for S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: workflow/tool event emission, retries, error state, checkpoints, logs, and grader/comparator/analyzer machinery bundled specifically inside the skill-development evaluation workflow. - Closure path: routine tool/workflow results feed the same operational path; skill-development graders/comparators feed S4 capability adaptation. Neither supplies a complementary current-operations audit channel returning findings to an S3 owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: independent graders can judge skill-development trials, but the audited claim there is prospective adaptation quality rather than current S1 operational accountability; moving that mechanism across functions would be a category error. - Evidence: crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/agent.rs, flock-data/skills/skill-creator/SKILL.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: develop and test reusable future Flock capability in response to capability needs and externally/future-relevant information, then return the resulting adaptation into the skill surface used by later operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a user&#39;s desired capability may not exist or an existing skill may perform poorly; relevant documentation, similar skills, best practices, edge cases, model/tool behavior, evaluation failures and changing triggering behavior create future-facing distinctions that should change the reusable capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the concrete skill design and revisions—workflow/instructions/resources, evaluation cases/assertions, changes suggested by trial transcripts/benchmarks, reusable helper scripts and triggering-description changes—then decide whether further adaptation iterations are warranted within the user&#39;s stated objective/feedback. - Decision owner: the active first-party Flock agent following the shipped skill-creator adaptation procedure. The user/Parent supplies the capability goal, may confirm intent/evals and can stop or accept the loop, but the agent develops, tests and revises the adaptation options rather than merely persisting a user-authored artifact. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: packaged skill-creator instructions/resources; research through available tools/MCP where useful; with-skill versus baseline trial runs; graders/comparators/analyzer; benchmark aggregation; human feedback; description-optimization loop; filesystem skill format; skill loader/discovery and packaged application skill resources. - External distinction: relevant external documentation, similar skills, best practices, available tool/model behavior and user-environment requirements can expose capability gaps or better approaches that are not contained in the current skill alone. - Future / prospective distinction: the loop asks whether a reusable skill will handle representative future tasks and trigger appropriately, comparing candidate capability against baselines/previous versions and held-out or expanded evaluations rather than merely repairing the current turn. - Adaptation option generated: a new or revised reusable SKILL.md plus supporting scripts/resources, evaluation design and triggering description selected by the agent from those distinctions and trial results. - Path back into current capability / S3: the selected skill is written or packaged into Flock-readable user/project/workspace/install skill locations; flock-skills subsequently discovers/loads it into later agent contexts, changing the capability available to present operational runs. - Closure path: capability need + relevant external/future distinctions → agent researches/designs a reusable skill option → with-skill/baseline trials and feedback expose performance distinctions → agent revises the skill/description/resources → resulting skill is written/packaged in a Flock-readable skill location → later agent runs load/use the changed capability. - Boundary reachability: skill-creator is under flock-data/skills, those resources are included in the Tauri application bundle, and flock-skills resolves the installed skills resource directory plus user/project skill directories. This is a shipped product capability, not repository-only CI/dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: user intent and acceptance constrain the adaptation, but the substantive future-capability option generation, diagnosis of trial failures, revision choices and reusable artifact construction are delegated to the agent. Deterministic loaders/eval scripts support and measure the loop rather than owning those adaptation decisions. - Evidence: flock-data/skills/skill-creator/SKILL.md, crates/flock-skills/src/paths.rs, crates/flock-skills/src/discovery.rs, flock-ui/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: generic loading/discovery of already-authored skills alone would not establish S4. Credit depends on the separately shipped adaptation procedure that actively researches/develops/tests/revises a capability and closes it back into the runtime-readable skill surface. Some evaluation branches depend on optional subagent/CLI facilities; the core draft → test/review → revise → persist loop remains explicitly adapted for environments without those facilities.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity- or ultimate-policy-level closure is established at the reviewed Flock recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: ordinary tool risk and human intervention are regulated, but no evidenced organizational identity/policy issue is routed to a legitimate ultimate authority whose decision returns as non-routine global policy for subsequent operation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for S5. - Decision owner: none established for S5 at this boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ToolApprovalManager, per-session Default/AutoEdit/Yolo modes, per-category “always” approval, sensitive-tool approval, human nodes/ask-human/request-human-assistance, sandbox permissions and workflow configuration. - Closure path: human/tool approvals can approve or deny an ordinary pending action and session modes can change which tool categories auto-approve, but that is task/session authorization rather than identity/ultimate-policy closure balancing current operations and future adaptation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: approval machinery enforces user/session choices; neither it nor an agent is shown owning legitimate ultimate organizational identity/policy authority. Ordinary human final say over a tool call does not meet S5 by itself. - Evidence: crates/flock-core/src/ipcinterface/approval.rs, crates/flock-workflow/src/nodes/agent.rs, crates/flock-tools/src/tools/builtin/askhuman.rs, crates/flock-tools/src/tools/sandbox/requesthumanassistance.rs. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/c8624b55eaa098004a856ac116b7881495866f8f/assessments/flock.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit c8624b55eaa098004a856ac116b7881495866f8f\">c8624b5 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-21T17:11:20Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xerrors/Yuxi\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Yuxi</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-21\">2026-09-21</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/xerrors/Yuxi/tree/86c67dc41249c3c9cad9e976fd8a006194d39a76\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 86c67dc41249c3c9cad9e976fd8a006194d39a76\">86c67dc &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: perform user-directed knowledge, analysis and artifact-producing work through a first-party model/tool execution loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user requests, retrieved knowledge, files/workspace state, tool/API results, sandbox execution outcomes, model/tool errors and changing conversation/task context. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select substantive next actions/tools and decide when available operational evidence is sufficient to continue, delegate or return a result. - Decision owner: the running Yuxi model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Yuxi ChatbotAgent, configured runtime tools, composite filesystem/sandbox backend, checkpointer, request/run worker lifecycle, retries, context summarization, Skills/MCP and knowledge integration. - Closure path: persisted request → prepared Yuxi agent graph → model-selected tool/action → first-party execution → ToolMessage/observation → subsequent model decision → final persisted result. - Boundary reachability: the standard Yuxi API/worker path directly instantiates and executes ChatbotAgent; no downstream custom harness is required to obtain the model/tool operational loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: LangGraph executes graph mechanics and external providers supply model inference, but the model actor owns the discretionary operational choices while Yuxi owns the executable loop, tools/context and durable run path. - Evidence: ARCHITECTURE.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/base.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/buildin/chatbot/graph.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: generic LangGraph/DeepAgents capabilities not assembled and exposed by Yuxi are not inherited.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes attenuation of a concrete interference, conflict or oscillation among at least two distinct Yuxi S1 units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S2 at the reviewed recursion. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for S2. - Decision owner: not established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: child AgentRun lifecycle, same-thread no-concurrent-write rule, FIFO request queues, run leases, shared runtime/workdir, parent cancellation and delegation exist but are not credited as coordination by topology or serialization alone. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the root can manage child commitments, but evidence inspected does not identify a concrete cross-child disturbance and an S2-specific attenuation relation whose feedback changes subsequent child behavior. - Evidence: ARCHITECTURE.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/subagenttask.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: shared workspaces can create potential interaction, but potential collision is not evidence that Yuxi ships a concrete S2 attenuation loop.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: regulate the current portfolio of subordinate Yuxi agent commitments during a root-agent run. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete, delayed, failed, unnecessary or still-running subordinate tasks; changing need for specialist work as child results arrive; risk of finalizing while required delegated work remains unresolved. - Decisive decision or feedback right: decide what child work to start, inspect current child status/progress, await results when needed, cancel a child, continue root work in parallel and condition subsequent commitments/finalization on current child state and returned results. - Decision owner: the root Yuxi model agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Yuxi SubAgent middleware; durable parent/child AgentRun relation; subagentstart, subagentstatus, subagentcancel, subagentawait; ChatBotState.subagentruns; child ownership verification; cancellation/request services; persistent run/result state. - Closure path: root identifies current need → starts one or more configured subordinate operations → child run state/result returns as ToolMessage plus subagentruns state → root inspects/awaits/cancels/launches further work as current conditions require → subsequent root action/final answer changes accordingly; remaining live children are cancelled when parent ends. - Boundary reachability: standard configured ChatbotAgent construction automatically installs the SubAgent middleware whenever visible/configured Yuxi subagents exist; the root receives the current-control tools directly in its normal model loop. - Whole-system current view: the root graph state accumulates structured summaries for each child run keyed by runid, including current status, agent identity, timing/error/artifact/result fields; explicit status/await calls refresh individual commitments before the root acts. - Current-control decision scope: start new subordinate commitments, continue prior child threads when idle, inspect their progress/results, wait for required completion, cancel no-longer-wanted work and decide whether the root can safely finalize. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: repositories/services enforce ownership and durable state, but the root model decides which current commitments exist and how their live evidence changes ongoing portfolio action. Removing the model leaves lifecycle mechanics but no discretionary whole-run allocation/control decision. - Evidence: backend/package/yuxi/agents/buildin/chatbot/graph.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/buildin/chatbot/state.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/subagenttask.py, ARCHITECTURE.md. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: this mapping is conditional on the standard supported mode with configured/visible SubAgents; a single-agent configuration still retains S1 but does not instantiate a multi-unit portfolio.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes a sufficiently independent complementary challenge to operational reality with corrective return into S1/S3. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S3. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model/tool audit events, Langfuse integration, run/debug timelines, knowledge/RAG evaluation, human tool approval, retries and child status/results all exist but serve ordinary execution, evaluation or parent governance. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: child outputs are production inputs to the root, not a structurally independent audit channel; dashboard/evaluation evidence does not by itself exercise corrective operational authority; human approval is Parent intervention rather than S3. - Evidence: ARCHITECTURE.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/toolapproval.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/subagenttask.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a separately configured reviewer subagent could be constructed downstream, but role naming or constructibility is not a shipped independent audit closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes autonomous or parent-governed prospective environment-facing adaptation of Yuxi organizational capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S4. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for prospective capability adaptation. - Decision owner: not established for S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: knowledge/RAG retrieval, persistent checkpoints, user Memory, dynamically activated Skills, configurable models/tools/MCP and reusable agent/subagent definitions broaden available capability but do not themselves decide and integrate a future organizational adaptation from environmental distinctions. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the memory middleware explicitly writes long-term memory only when the user asks it to remember; Skills are selected/configured resources and dynamic activation occurs within the present run. Persistence/reuse is not prospective S4 closure. - Evidence: README.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/memory.py, backend/package/yuxi/agents/middlewares/skills.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: administrators/users can change knowledge, agent, Skill, MCP and model configuration, but generic external configuration does not establish Parent-governed S4 without a qualifying S4 issue/option/decision/return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no material first-party path establishes identity- or ultimate-policy-level adjudication that closes unresolved present/future tension for the Yuxi organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: not established as S5. - Decisive decision or feedback right: not established for identity/ultimate policy. - Decision owner: not established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: multi-tenant RBAC, administrator configuration, agent prompts/descriptions, tool approval mode, model/resource visibility and human approvals constrain operation. - Closure path: not applicable. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: these are access/configuration and parent safety controls. The reviewed repository does not establish a function-specific identity/ultimate-policy issue and an authority that adjudicates it and returns the decision into operation. - Evidence: README.md, ARCHITECTURE.md, backend/package/yuxi/agents/toolapproval.py. - Basis: structural absence review. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: super-admin/operator authority is real product governance but does not become VSM S5 without the identity/ultimate-policy organizational function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f0e739507a67e4069c7ef24add9a14d2bfcea7ec/assessments/yuxi.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f0e739507a67e4069c7ef24add9a14d2bfcea7ec\">f0e7395 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-22T22:58:40+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AutoCodeRoverSG/auto-code-rover\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AutoCodeRover</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-22\">2026-10-22</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/AutoCodeRoverSG/auto-code-rover/tree/585d3e639aeda58ef0b6a151dd1cc2721a94d267\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 585d3e639aeda58ef0b6a151dd1cc2721a94d267\">585d3e6 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: investigate a supplied code issue and produce an applicable candidate patch through autonomous search and code-edit choices. - Disturbance / variety regulated: uncertainty about code locations, incompatible search requests, non-applicable diffs, failed reproduction and regression outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: select iterative search APIs and suspected bug locations, generate patch content, and revise attempts after code/tool feedback. - Decision owner: first-party search/patch model interactions; operator selects provider and issue, while deterministic backends execute code searches and validation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SearchBackend AST-aware calls, API-call proxy, diff extraction/applicability, optional SBFL and test execution, bounded retries and candidate selection. - Closure path: issue + repository → model-selected code search → returned source context → model-generated patch → applicability/test/reviewer feedback → next patch or output selection. - Boundary reachability: the main source-search/patch path is the default github-issue, local-issue and SWE-bench work mode and does not require the experimental reviewer. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-generated search instructions and patch content vary with observed task data; configured round ceilings, tool execution and user-provided issue are supporting restrictions, not owners of those decisions. - Evidence: search selects APIs and consumes outcomes, model-generated applicable patch and feedback, pipeline. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the shipped model/tool/patch control loop; no claim about the patch&#39;s external task success. - Caveats: live model-backed operation was not tested; ordinary validation is opt-in and some tasks fall back to patch-only generation.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: in the experimental reproducer/reviewer mode, dampen conflict between independently proposed issue reproducer and corrective patch by adjudicating which of the two must be revised. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a patch can make a faulty test pass, or a valid patch can seem wrong because the test incorrectly represents the issue; indiscriminate iteration on only one cell would perpetuate mismatch. - Distinct S1 units: TestAgent autonomously proposes executable reproducing tests; PatchAgent autonomously proposes code changes, each with a dedicated model prompt, artifacts and feedback/revision history. - Inter-S1 disturbance: their joint candidate outcome disagrees with the observed buggy/patched reproducer results and issue semantics, with the conflict potentially attributable to either test construction or patch construction. - Attenuating coordination relation: an independent reviewer model is given the issue, both artifacts and original/patched execution evidence and returns separate patch-correct / test-correct judgments and targeted advice. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: ReviewManager.generator routes patchdecision == NO to PatchAgent.addfeedback and writeapplicablepatchwithfeedback, while testdecision == NO updates TestAgent.addfeedback and writereproducingtestwithfeedback; the resulting artifacts are re-executed on the following round. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: the reviewer is explicitly asked to settle which of two distinct operational outputs is defective, and that decision changes which S1 producer receives a corrective instruction. Merely executing tests or selecting the last patch is not the credited S2 relation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: dynamically judge whether to correct the test, patch or both from cross-cell evidence; the Python manager routes the judgment. - Decision owner: first-party model reviewer in the opt-in mode, not the fixed CLI scheduler or a user-assembled external supervisor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: original/patched repro execution, two-sided review JSON, separate feedback histories, bounded review rounds and validation. - Closure path: test + patch candidates → execute both interpretations → reviewer locates mismatch → affected producer receives focused feedback → regenerated test/patch enters next comparison. - Boundary reachability: the first-party SWE-bench --reproduce-and-review switch installs this loop when a valid reproducer exists; it is experimental, off by default, and not exposed for general local/GitHub issue commands. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the correctness allocation and advice are discretionary reviewer-model output; the manager&#39;s branches only implement the model&#39;s two judgments. This does not imply shared-system scheduling, generic writer-conflict mediation or whole-current governance. - Evidence: flag and scope, mode gate, two-sided reviewer prompt, feedback dispatched to each agent, reproducer feedback memory. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium; the narrowly defined test/patch coordination closure is explicit, but no model-run has established effectiveness or a broader autonomous S2. - Caveats: opt-in experimental SWE-bench only; no reproducer means fallback to patch-only generation. The review loop is quality-focused, does not coordinate parallel workers or resource contention, and its S2 interpretation depends on treating the test and patch generators as distinct operational units rather than one monolithic coding S1.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: direct present resource, commitment, priority and accountability trade-offs of multiple operating units for the whole assessed harness. - Disturbance / variety regulated: model/task retries, concurrent batch workloads, failing patch attempts and tool execution time. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established at an S3-specific organizational level; task grouping, fixed retry limits and patch selection are preset process management. - Decision owner: human CLI/config author and deterministic orchestration, with model decisions local to repair tasks. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ProcessPoolExecutor, group sequencing, overall retry limit, model cycling, validation and generated patch selection. - Closure path: user-supplied task groups → parallel/serial processing → per-issue output; no separate current-wide negotiation/reprioritization and corrective allocation among an accountable whole. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: process limits and ranking candidate patches do not own whole-organization resource/commitment decisions. - Evidence: task group scheduling, bounded attempts and selection. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for the bounded first-party negative. - Caveats: a separate operator organization can add current management, but is outside this assessed runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: independently challenge an S3 current-management claim using a complementary route to operational reality, then return findings for corrective control. - Disturbance / variety regulated: potentially false patch-correctness claims and misleading local tests. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no such S3 audit right; the experimental agentreviewer decides whether local code/test artifacts are correct as ordinary repair-loop QA. - Decision owner: model reviewer and deterministic validator under S1/S2 repair production, not a distinct management auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: test execution against original/patched code, reviewer feedback JSON, optional regression validation and logs. - Closure path: code/test pair → reviewer/validator → revise task artifacts, not S3 claims → independent operational audit → S3 corrective management return. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the reviewer is model-owned but its object is the code patch and reproducer, not complementary verification of whole-current S3 management. - Evidence: reviewer&#39;s actual task, manager feedback scope, local patch validation. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for task-level QA versus S3 boundary. - Caveats: the term &quot;reviewer&quot; alone does not establish audit ownership at organizational recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: monitor wider future environment, propose adaptive changes and return selected options to current harness capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unfamiliar future issue classes, external library changes, provider variation and changing codebases. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no evidenced prospective organizational capability decision; model selection, fault localization and retrieval are for the present issue. - Decision owner: user/developers choose providers, install new methods and maintain the project; the repair model chooses present-task actions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model alternatives, AST search, issue context, regression feedback, retries and an external paper/benchmark. - Closure path: issue/code evidence → patch for the same issue, or configured provider fallback; not future trend sensing → strategic adaptation → returned capability change. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: solving a previously unseen issue is S1 operational adaptation, not an installed outside-and-then intelligence function. - Evidence: pipeline and model retry, public research and scope statement. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high for reviewed runtime. - Caveats: external researchers can measure and change future versions, but their decisions are outside the one-run harness boundary.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: settle ultimate purpose and binding identity/policy conflicts for the running organization. - Disturbance / variety regulated: requested edits outside intended scope, provider-policy rejection, task-local constraints and permissible execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party ultimate policy/identity decision path; user provides issues/models/options and model produces candidate patches subject to configured guards. - Decision owner: human operator/developer for values and configuration, with external provider policy enforced by provider. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: CLI tasks, prompt instructions, errors/exception fallback and test validation. - Closure path: user-supplied goal/config → issue repair attempt; no identity-level disagreement → legitimate ultimate authority → binding policy decision returned to the operating system. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompt-level do/don&#39;t rules and fallback on an external content-policy exception do not transfer supreme policy authority to the coding agent. - Evidence: CLI goal and model selection, provider-policy exception fallback, patch prompt scope. - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high for bounded negative at the local runtime. - Caveats: broader project governance and legal restrictions are external arrangements.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/3be8d80335ca7cbb047936f15ea0705c6d94f3dd/assessments/au/autocoderover.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 3be8d80335ca7cbb047936f15ea0705c6d94f3dd\">3be8d80 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-26T23:41:42+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OS-Copilot/OS-Copilot\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OS-Copilot / FRIDAY</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-26\">2026-09-26</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/OS-Copilot/OS-Copilot/tree/f720af8807e49a92dda64572d2c6bc6c0ac7ee7e\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision f720af8807e49a92dda64572d2c6bc6c0ac7ee7e\">f720af8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously plan and execute concrete computer/OS/application work needed to satisfy a user task, adapting task-local actions to returned execution state. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user goals, available tools, filesystem/application state, generated-code failures, missing intermediate operations, execution outputs/errors and changing prerequisites within a task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose/decompose the next substantive computer actions, generate or select executable tools, interpret returned execution state and decide whether to continue, amend or replan the active task. - Decision owner: first-party model-driven FRIDAY planner/executor/judge path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tool retrieval, dependency graph/topological ordering, Python/Shell/AppleScript/API execution environments, bounded repair iterations, task state, generated-tool repository and deterministic loop/control plumbing. - Closure path: user task -&gt; model decomposes/selects actions -&gt; generated/retrieved tool executes against OS/application environment -&gt; result/error/filesystem state returns -&gt; model judges/replans/repairs -&gt; changed action executes -&gt; task state updates. - Boundary reachability: quickstart.py and documented examples instantiate FridayAgent, while FridayAgent.run() directly invokes the credited planner/executor/refinement path. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic code transports state and executes selected code, while substantive task decomposition, tool generation, result judgment, amendment and replanning choices are model-driven. - Evidence: README.md; oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py; oscopilot/modules/planner/fridayplanner.py; oscopilot/modules/executor/fridayexecutor.py. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: the README notes single-round conversation, but the internal execution of one task is multi-step and result-conditioned; conversational turn count does not break the operational loop.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2 path regulating a concrete disturbance between distinct autonomous S1 units is established at the declared FRIDAY-installation recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: the planner orders dependencies among subtasks and modules exchange artifacts, but those subtasks/modules are components of one operational agent rather than separately accountable S1 units producing an inter-operation oscillation/interference problem. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for inter-S1 coordination. - Decision owner: none established for S2. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: dependency graph, topological ordering, prerequisite-result passing, shared tool repository and sequential subtask loop. - Closure path: task decomposition creates ordered work inside S1, but no distinct S1-A/S1-B interaction disturbance -&gt; coordinating judgment -&gt; changed later behavior of those S1 units loop closes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: sequencing dependencies is operational planning, not evidence of the VSM S2 organizational function. - Evidence: oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py; oscopilot/modules/planner/fridayplanner.py. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: a deployment containing several separately accountable FRIDAY instances could create an S2 problem, but that is a different system-in-focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party whole-system current-control function over multiple or separately managed operational units is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: task-local execution failures, weak outputs and missing prerequisites are regulated, but they are handled inside the same S1 episode that performs the work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no separate actor is evidenced with authority to form a whole-installation current view and intervene in the present operational organization beyond the active task-local plan/repair loop. - Decision owner: none established for S3; FRIDAY&#39;s planner/judge retains task-local S1 action authority. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: in-band judgetool, repair loop, replanning, task graph/status, score threshold and bounded repair count. - Closure path: execution state -&gt; same operational agent judges/repairs/replans -&gt; same task continues; this closes S1 recovery, not a separate management loop over the current whole. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: model-driven replanning is real discretion, but its organizational function is local task execution/recovery rather than whole-system current control. - Evidence: oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py; oscopilot/modules/executor/fridayexecutor.py; oscopilot/modules/planner/fridayplanner.py. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: names such as planner or orchestrating agent are not promoted to S3 without a whole-system current-view/current-control witness.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no first-party independent complementary-audit channel with distinct access/judgment and corrective return is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: FRIDAY evaluates generated code/output and may repair or replan, but this is ordinary in-band operational verification. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no independent auditor is evidenced with a separate claim-checking path over operational reality. - Decision owner: none established for S3; judgetool uses the ordinary executor module/model configuration inside the same task loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: LLM judgment prompt, execution result/error/filesystem observations, score threshold and repair/replan path. - Closure path: operation -&gt; same in-band executor/judge evaluates -&gt; same agent repairs/replans -&gt; operation continues; no independent complementary path returns findings to a separate corrective owner. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: semantic judging exists, but independence/complementary access required for S3 is absent. - Evidence: oscopilot/modules/executor/fridayexecutor.py; oscopilot/modules/basemodule.py; oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py. - Basis: explicit + structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: calling the component a judge or critic does not satisfy S3; no separate reviewer/model/provider/evidence channel comparable to an independent audit path is configured by the standard runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A - Function: generate and implement prospective reusable computer capabilities from external software/package/demo context so later operational tasks can perform work the current repertoire could not. - Disturbance / variety regulated: capability gaps exposed by unfamiliar software/application domains, new packages/APIs and task classes not covered by the existing generated-tool repository. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose a prospective course/lesson set for the selected external software/package context and, through lesson execution, generate reusable tools that expand the future action repertoire. - Decision owner: first-party SelfLearner model for course design plus the model-driven FRIDAY lesson execution path that produces qualifying reusable tools. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: software/package/demo inputs, prior-course history, model-authored course JSON, SelfLearning.learncourse, ordinary FRIDAY task execution, score threshold, persistent generated-tool files/JSON/Chroma vector database and retriever similarity search. - Closure path: operator identifies a capability domain -&gt; learner reads software/package/demo/prior-course context -&gt; model designs prospective lessons -&gt; FRIDAY executes lessons -&gt; successful generated Python capabilities are persisted -&gt; later ordinary task queries the same repository -&gt; newly learned tool descriptions/code are retrieved and influence execution -&gt; previously unsupported task becomes executable. - Boundary reachability: courselearning.py is a shipped first-party entrypoint documented by the self-learning tutorial; no downstream code changes or custom orchestration are required to create and reuse the adapted capability. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the operator selects the broad learning target, while the first-party model determines the course/lessons and FRIDAY generates/qualifies the executable tools; deterministic repository code only persists and retrieves those chosen capabilities. - External distinction: software/package context and optional demo-file contents describe capabilities outside FRIDAY&#39;s existing action repertoire; the documented example uses the external openpyxl library and an Excel file. - Future / prospective distinction: the learning mode is explicitly run to acquire tools for later tasks, rather than only repairing the presently failing task; its persisted output survives the learning episode and is queried in subsequent task planning/execution. - Adaptation option generated: a model-authored course of new capability lessons and the reusable generated tools produced by executing those lessons. - Path back into current capability / S3: qualifying tools are written into generatedtools.json, code/description files and the persistent vector index; ordinary FridayRetriever then automatically retrieves them for later S1 task planning/code generation, changing the executable repertoire without repository-source modification. - Evidence: courselearning.py; oscopilot/agents/selflearning.py; oscopilot/modules/learner/selflearner.py; oscopilot/modules/executor/fridayexecutor.py; oscopilot/toolrepository/manager/toolmanager.py; oscopilot/modules/retriever/vectorretriever.py; docs/source/tutorials/selflearning.rst. - Basis: explicit + structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: self-learning is a separate supported operating mode rather than automatically triggered from every failed task. That affects invocation, not ownership of the adaptation option once the mode is invoked. The broad learning domain/package remains operator-supplied and is not treated as autonomous S5 policy.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no first-party runtime identity/ultimate-policy adjudication loop is established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user task, model/provider settings, score/repair limits, available tools and self-learning domain inputs constrain behavior, but they are task/configuration/capability controls rather than organizational identity or ultimate-policy decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none established for defining/reconciling FRIDAY&#39;s identity, ultimate purpose or legitimate highest-level policy under competing operational/adaptation demands. - Decision owner: none established for S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: environment/configuration, prompts, thresholds, user task/learning inputs and static runtime structure. - Closure path: configuration/task inputs directly constrain S1/S4 behavior; no identity-level issue -&gt; legitimate ultimate authority -&gt; authoritative policy decision -&gt; returned governance loop is evidenced. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: FRIDAY exercises operational and capability-adaptation discretion, but not authority to redefine its own organizational identity or ultimate policy. - Evidence: README.md; oscopilot/modules/basemodule.py; oscopilot/agents/fridayagent.py; courselearning.py. - Basis: structural negative search. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: user-supplied objectives/learning targets are parent inputs, but ordinary task/capability target selection is not S5=P without an identity/ultimate-policy function.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/df12b45707a2cb0c30902dcd8b2df91a67f603c7/assessments/os-copilot.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit df12b45707a2cb0c30902dcd8b2df91a67f603c7\">df12b45 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T14:55:12+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentscope-ai/agentscope\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AgentScope 2.0</a><small>2024</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/agentscope-ai/agentscope/tree/5ff52f877de12d66a30d55af279dd4f42b1590f3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5ff52f877de12d66a30d55af279dd4f42b1590f3\">5ff52f8 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Configurable first-party model-directed reasoning/acting tool loop performs user/worker tasks and returns tool/environment feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown task conditions, tool execution responses/errors, context limits and external model results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose goal-specific tool calls, perform actions, interpret outcomes and continue or finish. - Decision owner: AgentScope-configured agent model in the SDK/Service&#39;s direct agent execution mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model adapters, Toolkit, middleware hooks, workspace/sandbox, permission gates, message/session store. - Closure path: User or leader prompt → agent reasoning → selected tool action → tool/environment result → model continuation and final worker report. - Boundary reachability: The published SDK and example Agent Service initialize real AgentScope Agent instances with toolkit/model and feed messages into replystream (README.md; src/agentscope/agent/agent.py; src/agentscope/app/service/chat.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model selects substantive task acts; host/session middleware only applies technical limits and transport policy. - Evidence: src/agentscope/agent/agent.py; src/agentscope/app/service/chat.py; src/agentscope/tool/toolkit.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: ? - Function: Candidate team message routing and session locking, but no proven function-specific first-party inter-S1 interference attenuation with returned feedback. - Distinct S1 units: Separately instantiated model-driven members of a first-party TeamRecord, each with its own session/task and message bus. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Potential conflicting simultaneous operations against shared task resources or overlapping workspace targets; the service&#39;s mere presence of separate sessions does not establish the specific disturbance regulation. - Attenuating coordination relation: The observed session lock serializes calls within one session; TeamSay routes messages and avoids misdelivery, but a function-specific inter-member contention/coordination policy has not been demonstrated. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: Team messages and leader reassignments exist; their connection to mitigation of a particular verified inter-worker conflict remains unproven. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: No positive S2 attribution is made from generic team topology, messaging, per-session locks or leader assignment alone. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Candidate inter-member resource contention, not established as an implemented control target. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Unresolved for interference-specific arbitration among independent worker sessions. - Decision owner: Potential leader or host service, not yet reconstructable for a material S2 relation. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MessageBus, TeamSay, sessionrun lock, team membership constraints, toolkit role filtering. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: No positive S2 owner is credited; the leader sends messages and the service serializes individual session turns, but neither path is proven to own inter-worker interference-specific decisions. - Closure path: Inter-agent messages are delivered; no defensible concrete collision → arbitration → modified workers path established. - Evidence: src/agentscope/app/tool/teamsay.py; src/agentscope/app/service/chat.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/teamcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/service/toolkit.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-led team leader makes current team staffing, delegation, repair and follow-up decisions based on worker reports. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Pending delegated tasks, changed worker outcomes, failures, information gaps and incomplete task progress. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Create a team, select role/worker, assign task, request clarifications and continue work after returned findings. - Decision owner: First-party hosted model leader using TeamCreate/AgentCreate/TeamSay, not a deterministic background task dispatcher. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: TeamRecord, role-separated team tools, agent/worker session allocation, message bus, report-enforcing middleware. - Whole-system current view: Leader receives team roster and completion/failure reports of the team it created; no claim of full multi-tenant service-wide control. - Current-control decision scope: Assign current work, change team tasks, re-instruct workers and integrate returned work in the bounded team recursion. - Closure path: Worker performs task and sends mandatory TeamSay report → leader model receives result → chooses follow-up or new task → worker session/action changes. - Boundary reachability: The Agent Service toolkit grants leader-specific team tools in configured leader mode; worker reports are actively enforced by middleware and persisted/routed back to the leader (src/agentscope/app/service/toolkit.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/agentcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/middleware/teammembermiddleware.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The actual choices of child role, scope and continuation are model-generated via shipped team tools; technical message delivery and individual session run locks are merely enforcement. - Evidence: src/agentscope/app/tool/teamcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/agentcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/teamsay.py; src/agentscope/app/middleware/teammembermiddleware.py. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: GoalPipeline provides separate executor/verifier agents and revision feedback, but complete independent operational-artifact challenge path is not established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: False executor goal-completion statements, missing required deliverables. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Verifier returns pass/fail about executor goal and an explanation, which may prompt another executor iteration. - Decision owner: Separately configured verifier Agent, subject to its actual tools/evidence access. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: GoalPipeline executor/verifier alternation, structured execution report, structured verification result. - Claim being audited: Executor&#39;s report that it fulfilled the original goal. - Ordinary reporting path: Executor returns a structured report under ExecutionReport. - Complementary access path: The verifier receives original goal plus executor&#39;s text report; supported independently sampled source/tool artifact inspection is not guaranteed by this pipeline contract. - Independence boundary: Distinct Agent object is possible, but materially independent observation from primary operational artifacts is unresolved. - Who acts on findings: Pipeline sends failure feedback back to executor, conditional on the verifier&#39;s structured judgment. - Closure path: Report → verifier model check → pass/fail → continue or return; missing independent observational basis prevents positive state. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: An independent model call is necessary but not sufficient when its only guaranteed input is the producer&#39;s own claim. - Evidence: src/agentscope/pipeline/goalpipeline.py; src/agentscope/app/middleware/teammembermiddleware.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Persistent memory, scheduling and middleware extension may support reuse across future tasks but do not yet establish an outside-and-then capability adaptation decision. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New task conditions, changing knowledge and external user contexts. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No confirmed owner/loop deciding a prospective adaptation option to future team capability. - Decision owner: Unresolved between user, configured agent, memory backend and developer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Long-term memory middleware, scheduler manager and goal/planning toolkit. - External distinction: External observations may be retained in memory, but this alone is not systemic intelligence. - Future / prospective distinction: Reuse is future-facing in time but not necessarily prospective organizational adaptation. - Adaptation option generated: Not established from the read-only source evidence. - Path back into current capability / S3: Memory/context or schedule modifications can affect future runs; full future intelligence → change is unproven. - Closure path: Captured memory → later prompt is technically supported, but no established S4 choice/feedback. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Memory retention and schedules are mechanisms, not proof of autonomous or parent S4. - Evidence: src/agentscope/middleware/longtermmemory; src/agentscope/app/manager/scheduler/schedulermanager.py; src/agentscope/tool/task/updatetask.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: Team identities, access/permission rules and human confirmations exist without established ultimate-policy identity decision/return loop at the bounded team recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Permissions over tools, resource access, multi-tenant rights and delegation. - Decisive decision or feedback right: The human/application owner sets permission rules and may approve actions, but ultimate organizational identity/policy closure is unresolved. - Decision owner: User/app role policies; not automatically a first-party agent policy controller or a qualified parent S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: PermissionEngine, TeamRecord roles, workspace permissions and HITL middleware. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Allowed identity, roles and acceptable operating permissions of configured team members. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: No S5-specific autonomous/parent owner established; execution-level rules are narrower than ultimate policy. - Return-to-operation path: Permission decisions guard specific tool calls and inheritance to child agents, not a proven changing-policy feedback loop. - Closure path: Permission request/policy → allow/deny action, lacking ultimate identity-level decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A policy engine enforces rules but does not decide the system&#39;s ultimate purpose or identity. - Evidence: src/agentscope/permission/engine.py; src/agentscope/app/tool/agentcreate.py; src/agentscope/app/service/toolkit.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/44d6c86f16e424c65ea333f05cf07553822f7db5/assessments/agentscope-2-0.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 44d6c86f16e424c65ea333f05cf07553822f7db5\">44d6c86 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T22:21:05Z\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/letta-ai/letta\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Letta</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/letta-ai/letta/tree/5bcdd177d70fa2b31a754cfcd801e77b2e1ab16a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5bcdd177d70fa2b31a754cfcd801e77b2e1ab16a\">5bcdd17 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: the primary agent owns model/tool action decisions and can delegate bounded work to first-party subagents.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: subagent launch, parallel execution and lifecycle tracking do not establish an autonomous anti-oscillation/shared-resource coordination decision right among peer S1 units.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the subagent manager schedules/delegates tasks but does not own whole-system current priorities, resources and commitments as a superior regulator.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:3\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: the default compaction-triggered reflection path launches a distinct autonomous background reviewer over already-completed primary-agent conversations. It searches for mistakes, contradictions and durable corrections and has an automatic integration path into parent memory/skills. This is a separate complementary audit/correction loop rather than ordinary self-reflection inside the S1 turn.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: reflection is retrospective internal learning from the agent&#39;s own conversations. It does not establish a distinct outside-and-then environmental intelligence function coupled to strategic adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: reflection may maintain memory/persona/skills, but ultimate identity, policy and authority remain constrained by parent/runtime configuration; editable memory is not legitimate S5 closure.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/ae8749aa005ea42801ed86e32476f5c417ecd9a0/assessments/letta.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit ae8749aa005ea42801ed86e32476f5c417ecd9a0\">ae8749a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-16T23:24:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MetaGPT</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-15\">2026-09-15</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/FoundationAgents/MetaGPT/tree/11cdf466d042aece04fc6cfd13b28e1a70341b1f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 11cdf466d042aece04fc6cfd13b28e1a70341b1f\">11cdf46 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: specialized role agents autonomously produce requirements, designs, code, analyses and other operational artifacts.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: the reviewed leader/SOP path primarily assigns and sequences work. No separate evidence shows a function that dampens destructive interference or oscillation among autonomous S1 units without centralizing their decisions.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: the default TeamLeader has a whole-team current view through team information, routed feedback and a structured plan; it can regulate current commitments by assigning specialists, closing tasks, changing/resetting the plan and gating downstream work on prerequisite results. This goes beyond worker selection alone and is exercised by an autonomous shipped role. Parent-authored roles, SOPs, prompts, budget and team composition constrain this loop but do not provide a separate first-party human/operator decision path over the same current whole-team commitments, so no (P) mode is published.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: review/testing inside the normal software-production path does not provide a materially independent alternative access channel to operational reality.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: planning, product/competitive artifacts and replanning are project work; the reviewed runtime does not establish a distinct external-and-prospective adaptation loop in two-way conversation with S3.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: role definitions, SOP, prompts, budget and team composition are parent/developer authored. No runtime agent holds legitimate ultimate identity/policy closure for the system-in-focus.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9/assessments/metagpt.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9ee1ea7472777767928c8b2ab035e0948a0467e9\">9ee1ea7 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-18T23:12:56+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lobehub/lobehub\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">LobeHub</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-18\">2026-09-18</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/lobehub/lobehub/tree/3af256caae11796da471bec5f57d882853f30f19\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3af256caae11796da471bec5f57d882853f30f19\">3af256c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: specialized agents autonomously perform assigned conversational, research, creation or tool-using work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: open-ended user tasks, domain-specific information, tool/model observations and task-specific uncertainty. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the substantive actions and output needed for the agent&#39;s assigned task or conversational role. - Decision owner: the invoked model-driven agent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent configuration, system role, model/provider selection, plugins, isolated async task context and session state. - Closure path: agent receives an instruction, performs work, returns a result to the group/user, and that result becomes input to subsequent orchestration or conversation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model-driven agent owns substantive task execution while LobeHub transports context/tools/results. - Evidence: README.md, docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, agent-management manifest/system role. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:1\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: A - Function: coordinate multiple specialized S1 agents by choosing dependent versus parallel work, agent selection, handoff order and group interaction mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: dependency-sensitive work that would be wrong in parallel, redundant/overlapping contributions, poor handoffs and unstructured multi-agent interaction. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose whether to use one agent, broadcast, sequential speaking, one async task or multiple parallel tasks, and choose the participating agents/instructions. - Decision owner: the model-driven Group Supervisor / Orchestrator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: speak, broadcast, executeAgentTask, executeAgentTasks, group orchestration runtime, shared group context and supervisor resume after member completion. - Closure path: supervisor analyzes the request, selects a coordination pattern and dispatches agents; member results return to the supervisor, which synthesizes, follows up or changes the next interaction. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: deterministic runtime executes the selected pattern, but the supervisor model owns the coordination discretion over mode, agent selection and follow-up. - Evidence: packages/builtin-tool-group-management/src/systemRole.ts, docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, group orchestration runtime. - Basis: explicit + structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: generic callAgent alone would not establish S2; the positive mapping depends on the explicit dependency/parallelism decision framework and returned supervisor loop. - Distinct S1 units: two or more specialized agents in an Agent Group, each capable of separate operational work. - Inter-S1 disturbance: dependent subtasks can be run in the wrong order, overlapping agents can add redundant work, and independent subtasks can be unnecessarily serialized; first-party guidance also calls out weak handoffs and overlapping roles as collaboration failures. - Attenuating coordination relation: the Group Supervisor chooses sequential, parallel, broadcast or focused modes and assigns work to agents according to dependency and expertise. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: member outputs resume the supervisor; it can ask follow-ups, synthesize, invoke another agent or terminate the group turn, thereby changing later contributions. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: first-party supervisor policy explicitly diagnoses dependence/parallelizability and selects an attenuation pattern for interactions among distinct operational agents, with returned member results driving further coordination.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no separate whole-group current-control function over resources, commitments, priorities or constraints is established beyond the S2 orchestration itself. - Disturbance / variety regulated: group task routing, model choice and current conversation state were inspected, but no distinct S3 current-control loop is established. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party owner was found with a whole-system current view and separate authority over group-wide commitments/resources/accountability. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S3. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: agent management, group profile editing, async task execution, scheduling and supervisor orchestration. - Closure path: no qualifying distinct S3 loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the orchestrator&#39;s evidenced rights are inter-agent task/mode coordination for the current group operation; naming it supervisor/operator is not enough to promote that same function to S3. - Evidence: group-management system role, Agent Group docs, agent-management tools. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: review another agent&#39;s produced work through a distinct reviewer/editor role and return critique into revision/re-review. - Disturbance / variety regulated: incomplete, unclear, inconsistent or low-quality outputs that require a second perspective before acceptance. - Decisive decision or feedback right: judge produced work, issue feedback and require/refine another contribution. - Decision owner: constructor path; LobeHub supplies explicit iterative/reviewer group patterns, but the developer/user must compose the reviewer role, independence and authority in the concrete group. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: iterative Agent Group mode, distinct agent identities, sequential handoffs, shared context and supervisor-mediated repeated turns. - Closure path: producer emits work; reviewer/editor returns critique; producer revises; reviewer can perform final review in the documented iterative pattern. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a separate model-driven reviewer can own the judgment once composed, but the standard distribution does not assign an invariant independent reviewer or audit authority automatically. - Evidence: docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, packages/builtin-tool-group-management/src/systemRole.ts. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium - Caveats: ordinary debate or synthesis is not counted as S3; the positive constructor claim is limited to the explicitly documented review/refine pattern with distinct roles. - Claim being audited: that another agent&#39;s draft/output is sufficiently correct, clear or complete for the group&#39;s purpose. - Ordinary reporting path: producer agent returns its draft/result through the normal group turn. - Complementary access path: a separately configured Editor/reviewer/QA agent receives the produced work and can critique it in an iterative group workflow. - Independence boundary: first-party group mechanics preserve distinct agent identities, but organizational independence/authority of the reviewer is supplied by the group composition rather than guaranteed by runtime policy. - Who acts on findings: the producing agent receives reviewer feedback and revises; the group/supervisor can route the revised result back for final review.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no separate external/prospective organizational intelligence loop is established at the Agent Group boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: user preference/history changes are learned, but no distinct future/environment adaptation function is closed. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party owner was found that senses external/future distinctions, generates adaptation options and returns a selected change into group capability/S3. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S4. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Personal Memory, editable structured memory, continual learning and tool/search capability. - Closure path: memory changes later agent context directly; no separate outside-and-then option-development loop is established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: personalization/learning and external tools do not automatically establish S4. - Evidence: README.md, Agent Group/runtime docs. - Basis: explicit + structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no identity/ultimate-policy governance function is established for the Agent Group. - Disturbance / variety regulated: group prompt, member roster and agent configuration can change, but no ultimate identity dispute/authority loop is evidenced. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no material first-party identity-level authoritative decision with a return-to-operation closure was found. - Decision owner: none established for qualifying S5. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: group profile, Agent Builder, agent CRUD, prompts, skills and user configuration. - Closure path: configuration edits alter operation, but they are not an evidenced S5 identity/ultimate-policy process. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: prompts and editable configuration are implementation/control artifacts, not proof of VSM S5. - Evidence: docs/usage/agent/agent-team.mdx, agent-management manifest/system role. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9f83e41179680c24cbe23a5091abda11fcc221d0/assessments/lobehub.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9f83e41179680c24cbe23a5091abda11fcc221d0\">9f83e41 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-10-12T16:19:31+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kyegomez/swarms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Swarms</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-10-12\">2026-10-12</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/kyegomez/swarms/tree/3009364a7e44dc3e55fec238b56b3a4fa6949a05\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 3009364a7e44dc3e55fec238b56b3a4fa6949a05\">3009364 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Model-directed individual first-party Agent solves a task via model/tool choice, observation and iterative continuation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Dynamic goals, changing tool outcomes, external model output, context and task errors. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose steps, call tools/subagents and decide when to continue or finish an operating task. - Decision owner: Per-agent hosted LLM/Agent runtime, not merely an agent-shaped tuple invoked by a deterministic sequencer. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent run, runautonomousloop, tool executor, model wrapper and workspace. - Closure path: Task → model-generated action/tool use → environment result → next model reasoning and eventual completion. - Boundary reachability: Python Agent.run and optional autonomous-loop configuration are used from the published distribution (README.md; swarms/structs/agent.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Context-sensitive model actions select operating acts; loop counters and provider wrappers do not own that choice. - Evidence: swarms/structs/agent.py; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: In bounded GroupChat mode, mediate multiple model-agent bids competing for a single shared conversational speaking slot, preventing speaker domination while retaining useful contributions. - Distinct S1 units: A configured GroupChat.agents set of independently invoked Agent objects, each producing a context-contingent (score, reply) via model-backed respond tool. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Several operating agents seek the same shared conversation floor simultaneously; unchecked publication produces competing turns, and repeated high-frequency speakers dominate the shared information channel. - Attenuating coordination relation: collectbids calls each agent, selectspeaker applies a shared threshold and recency penalty, chooses exactly one speaker, and post updates common conversation history seen by all peers on the next turn. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The selected speaker&#39;s contribution, not all conflicting bids, enters shared history for every agent&#39;s following model turn; recent speakers are penalized in future selections. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The explicit gate is driven by simultaneous claims to a scarce common conversational medium and recurring dominance, with a fairness attenuation policy and returned altered information environment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting simultaneous bids, message crowding and disproportionate conversational control. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select exactly one admissible turn among competing agent contributions. - Decision owner: First-party deterministic GroupChat floor arbiter, therefore C, not a separate agentic S2 decision owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: respond(score,message) tool, threshold, recency window/penalty, chosen-speaker posting and shared Conversation. - Closure path: Independently formed bids → contention/fairness gate → one allowed published contribution → next turn&#39;s changed conversation and adjusted future bids. - Boundary reachability: GroupChat.run uses the packaged bidding, selecting and posting operations; this is an opt-in published architecture, not an inactive helper (swarms/structs/groupchat.py; README.md). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Agents determine proposed content and scores; the decisive inter-agent floor grant is deterministic host policy (C). - Evidence: swarms/structs/groupchat.py; swarms/structs/conversation.py; README.md. - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Applies to GroupChat&#39;s shared conversation medium, not arbitrary shared file changes or all Swarms topologies. Does not claim broad resource conflict arbitration.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: A - Function: Model-led HierarchicalSwarm director assigns present worker orders and adjusts ongoing team work from collected subordinate results. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Divergent worker progress/results, uncertain task division, errors and changing completion quality. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Produce a model-derived order batch, choose which specialist performs what, inspect returned outputs and issue fresh orders in further loops. - Decision owner: First-party configured model director; deterministic parseorders and executeorders relay its decisions. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: HierarchicalSwarm director, OrderBatch parser, worker dispatch and optional feedback director, conversation storage. - Whole-system current view: Director sees the bounded swarm conversation/worker results and prior execution history, not every organization using Swarms. - Current-control decision scope: Current worker allocation, ordering, revisions and reattempts for the HierarchicalSwarm instance. - Closure path: Director plans and orders → specialized workers run → results return and enter conversation → director next loop reassigns/replans. - Boundary reachability: HierarchicalSwarm.run calls step, rundirector, parseorders, executeorders, and loops over result feedback when maxloops allows (swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py). - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Actual work orders originate in a model-led director within the shipped topology; loop counters and dispatcher enforce, not replace, task choices. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; README.md. - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: ? - Function: Some prebuilt modes include judge/verification or consensus phases; materially independent operating evidence inspection and correction path remains unresolved. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Invalid subordinate claims, low-quality task completion or conflicting agent assertions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Judge can evaluate outputs; whether it has independent primary-source evidence and authority over correction is not guaranteed. - Decision owner: Configured judge/consensus agent, not established as a truly separately observing auditor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Hierarchical judge toggle, MajorityVoting consensus and HeavySwarm verification-oriented roles. - Claim being audited: Specialist worker answer/output claims. - Ordinary reporting path: Worker results added to director conversation, majority-voting conversation or final report. - Complementary access path: Shipped judging paths chiefly pass worker outputs/derived conversation text; a separate direct observation of work artifacts has not been established at the first-party boundary. - Independence boundary: A different model/persona is not a guaranteed independent observations channel. - Who acts on findings: Directors or consensus can summarize and reconsider, but an independent audit finding → corrective operational reroute is unproven. - Closure path: Output → judge/consensus verdict → report or next loop, insufficient to classify a distinct S3 audit under the governing method. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Another LLM rating peer output is not equivalent to a complementary function sampling reality independently. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; swarms/structs/majorityvoting.py; swarms/structs/heavyswarm.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: ? - Function: Planning prompts, persistent workspace and memory may help later tasks but a decision owner adapting future systemic capability from external prospective observations has not been established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Future requirements, market or environment shifts and changing task complexity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Unresolved among user-supplied swarm config, model director and persisted task memory. - Decision owner: No uniquely identified first-party outside-and-then intelligence controller at the bounded swarm recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Conversation/workspace storage, planning role prompts, dynamic agent layouts. - External distinction: Outside facts can enter model context, but information access alone is not S4. - Future / prospective distinction: Multi-loop plans address current task commitments, not shown to adapt future organization capability. - Adaptation option generated: No verified capability-evolution choice produced by a distinct owner. - Path back into current capability / S3: Persistence and plan prompts can affect later runs, but external foresight → applied organizational redesign remains unresolved. - Closure path: Retained context → later work, incomplete future-intelligence and capability-change loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Prompted planning and memory are not enough for S4. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; swarms/utils/workspacemanager.py; swarms/structs/agent.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-unknown\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: ? - Function: Configured swarm identity, roles and execution limits exist but an ultimate organizational purpose/policy closure is not traced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: User boundaries, acceptable agent/tool behavior and system-level authority. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Developer/user chooses prompts, members and parameters; no proven ultimate-policy choice by a qualified first-party actor. - Decision owner: User-supplied configuration for role identity and deterministic safety checks; a complete parent policy mode remains unresolved. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system prompts, task role configuration and tool/platform restrictions. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Who chooses and revises the organization&#39;s overarching identity, purpose and final authority. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: Not established beyond out-of-band user/developer configuration. - Return-to-operation path: Prompt and parameter selection guides run behavior, but no complete governing policy feedback is proven. - Closure path: Configured goals/roles → worker execution; no verified policy challenge/ultimate revision returned to operations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A system prompt or fixed role roster is not itself an ultimate decision loop. - Evidence: swarms/structs/hiearchicalswarm.py; swarms/structs/groupchat.py; swarms/structs/agent.py. - Basis: unknown -\">?</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/5b1d5a9c5a355aedd4320e3898fd3b2af3f52800/assessments/swarms.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 5b1d5a9c5a355aedd4320e3898fd3b2af3f52800\">5b1d5a9 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-17T21:31:18+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/camel-ai/camel\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CAMEL Workforce</a><small>2023</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-17\">2026-09-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/camel-ai/camel/tree/8c791b7b9cf7deab56cb5a92818c34499af9097f\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 8c791b7b9cf7deab56cb5a92818c34499af9097f\">8c791b7 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: A. Worker agents autonomously execute assigned task work through model/tool loops.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: C. Workforce maintains explicit cross-task dependency state and gates release/order of work across multiple workers. This prevents dependent S1 work from proceeding against unsatisfied predecessor commitments, establishing a concrete coordination path beyond routing. The mechanism is supplied deterministically by Workforce while the application supplies the task graph, so ownership is constructor-side rather than agent-owned.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:2\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S3 / regulation: A. The Coordinator operates over shared workforce state, assigns work based on worker capability, tracks current commitments, handles failures, and can create new workers or redirect/replan work. These are whole-workforce current-control rights over assignments and available operational capacity. The model-driven coordinator owns the runtime choice within configured bounds.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: —. Quality/failure analysis evaluates returned task results in the ordinary Workforce control/reporting path. No materially complementary evidence-access channel or protected independent audit boundary was established. The historical C therefore does not survive current S3 criteria.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: —. Replanning and dynamic worker creation respond to current-task failure; they do not establish an external/future environmental intelligence loop that changes organizational capability.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: —. Human pause/stop/skip controls and the externally supplied objective are parent/operator controls over ordinary execution, not a demonstrated ultimate-policy/identity closure path. The historical P is therefore removed.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/cfe27303e117e1fd3a79fc8f485f33caada591a4/assessments/camel-workforce.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit cfe27303e117e1fd3a79fc8f485f33caada591a4\">cfe2730 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr class=\"has-a-combo\" data-combo=\"2\" data-a=\"2\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-09-19T19:14:57+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/micro/go-micro\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Go Micro</a><small>2015</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"2\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>2</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-09-19\">2026-09-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/micro/go-micro/tree/792b18c3010715e2f12621f0120bf058be225482\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 792b18c3010715e2f12621f0120bf058be225482\">792b18c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: autonomously transform user/task state through a model → tool/service/delegation → observation loop until an operational result is produced. - Disturbance / variety regulated: heterogeneous user goals, changing service/tool availability, tool/RPC/A2A results, persistent conversation state, provider responses, tool failures and intermediate observations requiring context-sensitive next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next service/custom/built-in tool, decide whether to plan or delegate, consume returned observations, continue the loop or settle the answer. - Decision owner: the active Go Micro LLM agent in NewAgent or micro chat direct-service mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: model adapters, service registry/tool discovery, built-in plan/delegate/request-input tools, memory/store, tool wrappers, step/loop/spend limits, approvals, checkpointing, retry/timeout logic, RPC/A2A/MCP integration. - Closure path: user/assigned task + current context → agent selects action/tool/delegation → Go Micro executes/gates it → tool/service/sub-agent result returns into model context → agent selects another action or final result. - Boundary reachability: NewAgent and micro chat are shipped, documented first-party runtime modes; the positive S1 loop is implemented in agent/ and CLI runtime code rather than inferred from examples or repository dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: removing the model-driven agent while retaining registry, memory, tool execution and guardrails leaves no contextual actor choosing the next operational action. Deterministic wrappers constrain and transport the model&#39;s decisions rather than replacing them. - Evidence: README.md, agent/builtin.go, cmd/micro/chat/chat.go. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: external model inference and remote A2A peers are substrate/environment. The positive state is established independently by the first-party agent loop around those dependencies.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no material first-party S2-specific loop was established that regulates a concrete interference/conflict/oscillation among distinct Go Micro S1 agent units at the declared product-runtime recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate paths inspected included multiple registered agents, RPC/A2A handoffs, service discovery/load balancing, durable flows, duplicate delegate/retry protection and shared registry/store infrastructure. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no qualifying S2-specific decision right is supplied. Delegation selects another actor for a subtask; service discovery/load balancing routes calls; idempotency/retry controls duplicate side effects; flow edges order work. - Decision owner: none established for S2 in the reviewed standard product-runtime boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registry, client load balancing, broker/store, plan/delegate tools, A2A/MCP transport, flow sequencing, retry/idempotency guards and isolated ephemeral sub-agent context. - Closure path: no first-party path was found that detects a specific interaction disturbance among two distinct S1 agents, selects an attenuation response for that disturbance, and feeds the coordination result back into subsequent local S1 behaviour. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multi-agent reachability, routing and delegation are present, but the Methodology explicitly requires more than communication/handoff. The reviewed product runtime does not supply an S2-specific autonomous or constructor path tied to a concrete inter-S1 oscillation/conflict witness. - Evidence: README.md, agent/builtin.go, gateway/a2a/a2a.go. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no distinct first-party whole-system current-control function was established beyond local agent planning/delegation and deterministic runtime enforcement. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate current-control paths inspected included store-backed plans, delegation, flow execution state, registry discovery, tool approvals, spend/step/loop limits and runtime stop/resume mechanisms. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the ordinary agent may revise its own task plan and delegate subtasks, but no separate product-runtime path was found with a whole-system current view plus authority to bargain/regulate shared resources, commitments or priorities on behalf of multiple operational units. - Decision owner: none established for S3 at the declared recursion. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: plan memory, delegate routing, flow state/checkpoints, registry/client machinery, tool/step/loop/spend limits, approvals and cancellation/timeouts. - Closure path: no S3-specific whole-system feedback/intervention loop was established. Local plan updates return to the same agent&#39;s task execution; deterministic limits enforce preselected constraints. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a parent agent that decomposes one user request and delegates one subtask is not automatically S3. The reviewed evidence does not show a standard mode where an agent receives a whole-system operational view and owns ongoing resource/commitment intervention across independent S1s. - Evidence: agent/builtin.go, README.md, flow/. - Basis: structural - Confidence: high - Caveats: .github/loop has project-level planning/current-work behavior, but it belongs to the excluded repository-development organization and is not borrowed into the product-runtime S3 mapping.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: challenge a flow-step result through a distinct verification judgment and return rejection feedback into another operational attempt before the flow proceeds with the bounded result. - Disturbance / variety regulated: plausible but deficient step outputs that ordinary body execution would otherwise return without a separate grade-and-correction pass. - Decisive decision or feedback right: the Grader decides pass/fail and supplies corrective feedback; Verify threads rejection feedback into the next body attempt and records the final verification outcome. - Decision owner: Go Micro does not ship a sufficiently independent default autonomous auditor for the general case. LLMGrader reuses the flow model and only sees the latest result. The application must compose a grader with independence appropriate to the audited claim; Go Micro supplies the S3-specific verification/feedback closure. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: bounded Verify attempt loop, feedback-field injection, optional backoff, verification metadata, flow checkpoint/result state and optional LLMGrader helper. - Closure path: body produces candidate result → composed grader audits it → rejection/feedback enters the next body input → body retries → accepted or bounded-failure result becomes the flow state. - Claim being audited: whether the current flow-step output satisfies the deployment-defined rubric/ground truth before it is accepted as the step&#39;s result. - Ordinary reporting path: the body step returns its State as normal flow output. - Complementary access path: the first-party Grader seam invokes a separately composed grading actor after body completion; the deployment can give that actor external ground truth, raw artifacts or a distinct model/service, and Go Micro closes its verdict back into retry. - Independence boundary: independence is not guaranteed by the built-in LLMGrader; it is intentionally left to the developer-supplied Grader. This is why the state is C, not A. - Who acts on findings: flow.Verify deterministically reinjects grader feedback into the next body attempt; the operational body/agent then produces the corrected candidate. - Boundary reachability: flow.Verify, the Grader contract and feedback/retry closure are shipped public first-party flow primitives at the reviewed revision, not CI-only tests. A deployment only needs to supply the independent grading actor/ground-truth access. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the function-specific audit path and return loop are first-party, but the decisive sufficiently independent audit actor is not supplied in a standard autonomous mode. General custom-code extensibility is not the basis; the credit is for the dedicated Verify/Grader primitive whose semantics are verification judgment plus corrective return. - Evidence: flow/verify.go, flow/verifytest.go. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: medium-high - Caveats: same-model LLMGrader alone is not treated as sufficient complementary independence. A deployment that wires only that helper may not operationally realize S3 even though the first-party constructor exists.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><span class=\"combo-lightning\" style=\"--gap:4\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 28\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\"><polyline class=\"lightning-glow\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline><polyline class=\"lightning-bolt\" points=\"0,14 12,9 22,17 34,6 45,16 57,10 68,19 80,7 90,16 100,14\"></polyline></svg></span><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a combo-a\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: A(P) - Function: detect that current operational capability cannot satisfy an external requirement, develop a service-level adaptation, materialize it, and return the new capability into subsequent agent operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: new user needs not covered by existing registered services/tools; requests to extend or modify an existing system; design/code/compile failures encountered while materializing the capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Base mode — the micro chat agent decides that no existing service can handle the request and chooses microgenerateservice; the generation models choose the service architecture/handler changes. Parent mode — the user reviews the LLM-designed system proposal and decides at Generate? [Y/n] whether the adaptation should be materialized. - Decision owner: Base mode — the autonomous chat/generation model path. Parent mode — the human operator owns the decisive accept/reject adaptation judgment, while the generator autonomously develops and implements the approved option. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: registry/tool discovery, generate.Design, existing-service scan, deterministic service-structure generation, LLM handler generation, bounded compile-fix loop, process startup, registry wait and tool refresh. - Closure path: external capability request + current service inventory → missing-capability distinction → model designs service adaptation → autonomous choice or parent approval triggers generation → code is written/compile-fixed/built/started → registry/tool set refreshes → subsequent agent turns can use the new service. - External distinction: the user asks for a capability absent from the current registered service/tool environment, or asks to extend/modify the existing system. - Future / prospective distinction: Go Micro models a desired future system/service capability relative to the current inventory rather than only selecting among existing tools; Design explicitly receives existing services and constructs the complete extended target design. - Adaptation option generated: one or more new/modified Go Micro services with schema/endpoints, generated handler/business logic and an updated managing agent/tool surface. - Path back into current capability / S3: generated services are compiled and started, allowed to register, micro chat refreshes discovered tools, and the new endpoints become immediately usable by the same operational agent; in initial generation mode the approved system becomes the subsequent running service/agent environment. - Boundary reachability: both micro chat autonomous missing-capability generation and micro run --prompt / CLI user-approved generation are shipped documented CLI modes. The positive mapping does not depend on repository CI or .github/loop dogfood. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: in base mode removing the model&#39;s missing-capability judgment/service-design choices leaves only a generator with no contextual adaptation decision. In parent mode the human decides whether the proposed adaptation is admitted, and that decision is returned through the same generator into executable capability. - Evidence: cmd/micro/chat/chat.go, cmd/micro/cli/generate/generate.go, cmd/micro/run/run.go, README.md, flow/analyze.go. - Basis: explicit - Confidence: high - Caveats: the adaptation path is developer/runtime oriented rather than production hot-patching of an arbitrary deployed service; the credited mode is nevertheless first-party and explicitly returns generated service capability into the supported running micro chat environment. flow.Analyze/LLMOptimizer alone would be only a partial adaptation constructor because applying its prompt proposal is caller-owned.\">A(P)</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no product-runtime identity/ultimate-policy decision loop was established at the declared recursion. - Disturbance / variety regulated: candidate paths inspected included agent system prompts, auth/rules, tool approvals, spend/step/loop limits, requestinput, generated-service confirmation and repository North Star/maintainer restrictions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: none of the product-runtime mechanisms escalates a genuine identity/ultimate-policy issue to a legitimate ultimate authority and returns that decision to govern subsequent operation at this recursion. - Decision owner: none established for S5 in the assessed product runtime. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: auth rules/JWT wrappers, guardrails, tool approvals, static prompts/configuration, human input pauses, spend budgets and generation confirmation. - Closure path: ordinary task/action approvals and configuration changes can alter execution, but no identity/ultimate-policy issue → legitimate authority → authoritative decision → returned policy loop was found. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: policy text, permission checks and human approvals bound ordinary operations; they do not by themselves own system identity or ultimate policy. Repository NORTHSTAR and off-limits maintainer decisions belong to the excluded repository-development organization. - Evidence: agent/builtin.go, auth/, internal/docs/CONTINUOUSIMPROVEMENT.md. - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9a9faa2e1208ecb2712c492398de7b7e7f24976a/assessments/go-micro.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9a9faa2e1208ecb2712c492398de7b7e7f24976a\">9a9faa2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T19:21:01+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Breno-J-Oliveira/coding_agent_harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Coding Agent Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Breno-J-Oliveira/coding_agent_harness/tree/7289baab4ada4e8b7d4ff5ba6e81d7bd0f99a05a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 7289baab4ada4e8b7d4ff5ba6e81d7bd0f99a05a\">7289baa &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform a delegated engineering task in the project under stated acceptance criteria. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different project states and failed tool actions require task-local judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose code and validation actions for the current work item and decide whether results satisfy the supplied criteria. - Decision owner: Coding-agent model instantiated by the shipped runner or the documented interactive host. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Board-to-prompt compilation, workspace write policy, log and JSON outcome parser. - Closure path: The host model receives tool outcomes; its structured report returns to the board and determines whether the work enters review or failure. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The launch path supplies an actual task-adaptive model turn; the Python runner enforces state and scope. - Boundary reachability: Normal board run reaches the host turn through the first-party invocation, and the installed interactive workflow feeds back into the same board. - Evidence: Board execution and outcome · Installable workflow · Operating guide - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Regulate interference among active peer S1 tasks. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Work items can share a project and depend on each other. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No dedicated peer-interaction decision right is supplied at the board boundary. - Decision owner: The board deterministically serializes work. Profiles are callable roles on the host. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Board locking, dependency edges and one ready item at a time. - Closure path: A terminal acceptance can unlock a later sequential item. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Role descriptions and sequential gates do not establish an actor or composition path that attenuates a concrete ongoing sibling interaction. - Evidence: Ready and lock rules · Delivery guidance · Integration profile - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Decide whether current project commitments can advance and which stalled work to re-open. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A review, failure or incomplete acceptance can hold up the whole active board. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Given the project-wide board, choose whether to accept evidence, retry a task or add current work with dependencies. - Decision owner: Project owner/operator in the managed-board mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Whole-board status, mutating commands, evidence hashing, gate enforcement and budgets. - Closure path: Owner acceptance marks a task done and releases its downstream dependencies; retry returns selected work to pending. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runner returns evidence and applies fixed limits. The operator can interpret and change the current work portfolio. - Boundary reachability: The normal board is read through status, then the same CLI persists accept/retry/add and the next run reads that state. - Whole-system current view: One project board across pending, failed, in-review and accepted commitments and their dependencies. - Current-control decision scope: Operator changes live commitments by accepting, retrying or adding work across the board. - Evidence: Board commands and gates · Board operator flow · Delivery release rules - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: The optional verifier can advance authorized items through its predicate. It does not exercise general cross-board supervisory discretion.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Corroborate an agent&#39;s ready claim before it releases more work. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A successful host exit and ready report can omit missing acceptance evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Independently test the trusted acceptance condition chosen for the task. - Decision owner: Project-authored external verifier controls the result after composition; no shipped semantic verifier owns that verdict. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: External-file requirement, pre-run identity snapshot and post-run hash check, separate process/log, explicit task opt-in. - Closure path: A passing verifier changes review to done and admits later eligible work. Failure keeps review and halts advancement. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The harness has a specific independent verification and return contract. The configured project verifier supplies the actual criterion and evidence acquisition. - Boundary reachability: The shipped advance entry point invokes the separate verifier on a task explicitly marked auto-verifiable. - Claim being audited: A completed task&#39;s ready claim against its acceptance contract. - Ordinary reporting path: The worker&#39;s normal structured report moves the board into review. - Complementary access path: The configured separate Python verifier receives project location and criteria and can inspect actual work directly. - Independence boundary: It is supplied outside the assessed project and hash-bound independently of the worker report, subject to operator configuration. - Who acts on findings: The board runner marks it accepted only on verifier success and otherwise prevents continuation. - Evidence: Advance gate · Verifier process and receipt · Reviewer boundary - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Independent acceptance is reconstructed only for a properly composed verifier. The harness does not certify its coverage.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: P - Function: Decide whether to add a new external skill to the project-local capability set. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Third-party candidate revisions bring new workflows, dependencies or side effects. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Accept an externally sourced candidate after reviewing its concrete provenance, scans and intended role. - Decision owner: Project owner/operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Candidate review workflow, local scanner, provenance/hash checks and explicit activation. - Closure path: Accepted candidate is copied into the project skill surface and the installed revision is recorded for future use. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The shipped workflow supplies observations and checks while activation remains under the responsible operator. - Boundary reachability: Bootstrap ships the intake workflow and the project skill installation path publishes selected skills into the supported hosts. - External distinction: A candidate external skill from a specific source revision, with observed files, behavior and local scan result. - Future / prospective distinction: Admitting it changes future project work capability. - Adaptation option generated: Review an exact candidate, retain it in isolation, or authorize installation with its provenance. - Path back into current capability / S3: Activation places reviewed content in the host&#39;s project skill directory for subsequent turns. - Evidence: Intake workflow · Scanner workflow · Project activation - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: This is an on-demand skill intake route. The deterministic stack recommender supplies no autonomous external adaptation decision.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Decide enduring identity and ultimate policy of the delivery workflow. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Project priorities and irreversible publication can require human authorization. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied separate identity-level review and authoritative return path was established. - Decision owner: Operator supplies goals and authorization. Repository author and host configuration set other defaults. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Bootstrap instructions, human-facing delivery policy, execution ceilings and task-level acceptance. - Closure path: Board and host apply current project instructions and task authorizations. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Human task acceptance and publication decisions remain within the already defined delivery mission. - Evidence: Bootstrap instructions · Board task contract · Director profile - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: The project owner can set external policy. That alone does not close a shipped S5 process.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9e4c51afc88af2b73c04f2bbf5b64da0c17081dc/assessments/co/coding-agent-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9e4c51afc88af2b73c04f2bbf5b64da0c17081dc\">9e4c51a &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T19:15:03+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aadhithya/featherweight\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">featherweight</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/aadhithya/featherweight/tree/fc27810cc57580b9fa2fad41c311ca9abc22927c\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision fc27810cc57580b9fa2fad41c311ca9abc22927c\">fc27810 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute an application-defined task as a model-directed turn or tool loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Uncertain task input and intermediate outcomes from tools and nested work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide the next allowed action and produce or revise the output. - Decision owner: The model-backed agent instantiated from the host declaration. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Framework-bound tools, schema validation, context, retry and result handling. - Closure path: Agent messages and tool results feed subsequent choices. Host sees valid output or a terminal failure. - Boundary reachability: AgentRunner implements the normal packaged entry point and creates the loop with the host-supplied model and the framework&#39;s declared tool constraints. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The agent chooses how to act within the typed interface. The framework builds and guards the loop. - Evidence: src/featherweight/runner.py, src/featherweight/runner.py, src/featherweight/models.py. - Basis: Explicit -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Regulate interactions between distinct operative agent units. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Parallel agent work can interfere through shared resources or duplicated work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied agent-level interference arbitration decision path was established. - Decision owner: None established at the organizational assessment boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Launch limits, semaphore handback, background completion delivery, and worker leases regulate execution mechanics. - Closure path: A waiting parent returns its execution slot and reacquires it after children finish. A worker whose lease is lost stops execution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The platform prevents runtime pool deadlock and duplicate execution claims. Host policy still supplies any discretionary coordination of scope, competing outputs, or substantive shared resources. - Evidence: src/featherweight/runner.py, src/featherweight/runner.py, src/featherweight/background.py, src/featherweight/worker.py. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: Medium - Caveats: The deterministic parent/child slot handback is an explicit resource safeguard. The assessment treats it at runtime execution recursion.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Control current commitments across the deployed agents or workers. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Job failures, cancellations, and demand exceeding worker capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No whole-deployment current operating view connected to substantive discretionary intervention was established. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The host can read job-specific status and cancel it. Worker queue leases and counters drive bounded enforcement. - Closure path: Cancellation, replay and lease loss produce job state changes. A host can compose decisions from this information. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Worker limits and failover enact predetermined rules. Job status and cancellation alone do not establish a whole-system current-control owner and scope. - Evidence: src/featherweight/client.py, src/featherweight/worker.py, src/featherweight/worker.py, docs/submitted-jobs.md. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independently audit actual work and feed findings into correction. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A schema-valid report can misrepresent what was done. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No packaged independent evidence judgment path with direct primary operational access was established. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Typed output checks, developer callbacks, attempt traces, and retry are built in. - Closure path: A checkoutput failure reaches a new retry attempt, with the decision originating in the host-supplied validator. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The supplied API evaluates the submitted input and output through the agent instance. Traces carry ordinary execution reporting. An independent evidence source and audit role would come from new host code. - Evidence: src/featherweight/models.py, src/featherweight/runner.py, src/featherweight/otel.py, docs/observability.md. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Sense future-relevant external changes and decide a capability adaptation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing provider and environment conditions can render a deployment&#39;s setup unsuitable. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied prospective intelligence-to-capability return path was established. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider settings, skill and MCP discovery, result reuse and caller-defined pipelines. - Closure path: Configured tools are found on demand and cached results may resume work. No installed adaptation policy follows from these returns. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool search and model settings help execute the current task. The document-learning example transforms sample inputs within its application purpose. - Evidence: src/featherweight/modelsettings.py, src/featherweight/registry.py, src/featherweight/skills.py, examples/doclearn/README.md, docs/example-pipeline.md. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Resolve ultimate identity or standing policy questions for the assessed harness deployment. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Competing purposes and authorities could change the deployment&#39;s governing mandate. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No supplied route for a runtime-originating policy or identity issue to reach an ultimate owner and return as a governed resolution was established. - Decision owner: None established at the credited deployment boundary. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent classes declare prompt, version, tools and eligible subagents, and the framework assembles the resulting system prompt. - Closure path: Developer definitions govern their instantiated agents. The library has no first-party decision return loop for settling mandate conflicts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The developer&#39;s deployment definition constructs its actors. Permission by declared class and static injected prompt parts are enforcement of design choices. - Evidence: src/featherweight/models.py, src/featherweight/models.py, src/featherweight/runner.py, docs/agents.md. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/9289ae4f4fb54c04cbd8b2166c9d0f30ffa04194/assessments/fe/featherweight.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 9289ae4f4fb54c04cbd8b2166c9d0f30ffa04194\">9289ae4 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"1\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-19T21:38:44+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/showlab/ForceHarness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ForceHarness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>1</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-19\">2026-11-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/showlab/ForceHarness/tree/733407c1ea90d8b474dff7809d9349d0f5f0b1d5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 733407c1ea90d8b474dff7809d9349d0f5f0b1d5\">733407c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: complete a physical manipulation/investigation task through model-selected tools. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown object/contact properties, failed actions and changing physical outcomes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose the next primitive/arguments or done after physical observations. - Decision owner: API model in LLMAgent or monitor model bridge; manual/scripted sources do not establish A. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: primitive controllers, validation, estimators, images, relay, history and guards. - Closure path: Episode appends ContactReports as tool results; real runner validates a relay call, executes and returns receipts/scene into the next model turn. - Boundary reachability: native episode/real runner and monitor bridge wire model selection to supplied tools without custom autonomous policy code. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the model selects task-dependent actions; motor control and formula-based estimators enforce/describe them. - Evidence: Episode/LLMAgent, tool schemas, real runner, model/operator relay, primitives - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: human grasp/release permission can constrain actions without choosing the complete solution. No model/simulator/robot was run.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: prevent distinct episode work cells from overlapping control of one real arm/scene. - Disturbance / variety regulated: a new live-episode request can compete with an already running cell for the same robot and unreinitialized scene. - Decisive decision or feedback right: admit/defer a requested episode until shared hardware and scene handover are ready. - Decision owner: operator/developer composition; no installed autonomous episode coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: SessionManager active-live registry, guarded launch planning and terminal states. - Closure path: conflicting live launch is refused as already running; after terminal state and operator scene reset/checklist, a new checked launch can proceed. - Boundary reachability: the shipped monitor checks existing live sessions before launching the native runner; the real-mode handover path is hardware-specific. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: fixed exclusion is S2 support, not an autonomous owner. A developer would still compose an actor around the supplied admission/feedback path. - Evidence: monitor SessionManager, monitor control routes, monitor modes - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: only monitor-managed live launches share this check. Independent monitors/robot clients are not shown to share a global lock; manual scene reset is required.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: operator control over monitor-managed current episode commitments and shared robot use. - Disturbance / variety regulated: active/queued work may need ending, interruption or recovery after inspection of progress and current physical/model state. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose Finish next turn, Interrupt or controlled Force quit for selected current work, cancelling queued continuation. - Decision owner: authorized local monitor operator; no autonomous current-portfolio controller. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: session registry, live force/action/scene view, bridge state, process handles and authenticated stop route. - Closure path: stop cancels a queued successor, requests relay finish or interrupts the active runner; native cleanup stops control and persists/returns updated session status. - Boundary reachability: monitor is a shipped live operating console with current state and targeted intervention, beyond historical experiment charts. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the human selects current intervention. Signals, budget caps and motor guards enforce it; per-grasp approval is not the S3 witness. - Evidence: monitor SessionManager, monitor control routes, live state/force view, monitor modes, real runner - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: P is the monitor-operated deployment, not bare Episode or the whole research organization. Soft Interrupt is not an emergency-stop certification.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no supplied complementary audit with a corrective operational return. - Disturbance / variety regulated: claimed success may differ from simulator truth or operator visual judgment. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no packaged independent audit judgment entering subsequent control. - Decision owner: benchmark scorer/operator labels are evaluation; no runtime S3 owner established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: ground truth, contact QA, transcripts, reports, videos and monitor labels/comparison. - Closure path: true/claimed success is saved after the task; monitor labels do not modify original episode records or route corrective agent control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: alternative ground truth exists, but terminal scores/charts/labels alone do not close runtime S3. - Evidence: Episode/LLMAgent, live state/force view, monitor modes, labels - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: external researchers can audit these records, but their actor and closure are not supplied by the assessed runtime.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supplied prospective external adaptation of future harness capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: current force/contact differences are handled within the manipulation task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no future-capability option selection and adaptation return. - Decision owner: model chooses current actions; developers/operators choose calibration and offline research variants. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: tare/settle, fixed estimators, context, task prompts, static playbooks and offline learned baselines. - Closure path: measurements change the next primitive choice; no prospective capability-library/option/admission loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: external force response and bias calibration are current operations, not automatically S4. - Evidence: architecture, fixed estimators, primitives, Episode/LLMAgent, offline baselines - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: frozen model weights alone do not justify absence; the missing relation is prospective organizational adaptation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy issue-resolution loop. - Disturbance / variety regulated: physical risk, force/speed/workspace limits and consent bound episodes. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/policy escalation and authoritative returned governing resolution. - Decision owner: operator approves actions and developer fixes guards, without an established S5 function. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: system/task prompts, validation, guard, startup checklist and fresh grasp/release confirmation. - Closure path: consent/refusal becomes tool feedback or execution constraint; no identity/ultimate-policy adjudication governs later operation. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: human go-ahead, safety veto and static guard are not themselves S5. - Evidence: model/operator relay, robot guard, real runner, validation, repository boundary - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: lab/institution governance cannot be inferred from ordinary local operating permission.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/f749c8dfed2c3c71f08ad9f924d777c0a755bfa3/assessments/fo/forceharness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit f749c8dfed2c3c71f08ad9f924d777c0a755bfa3\">f749c8d &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T19:29:00+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ProMetaDev/ui-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UI Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/ProMetaDev/ui-harness/tree/a20efcec26292af8457cb372b4f657f53574eda2\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision a20efcec26292af8457cb372b4f657f53574eda2\">a20efce &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Perform goal-directed tool work and return results under the workspace rules. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changing inputs, failed calls and workspace effects require task-local decisions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose tools, interpret results and decide a subsequent action or final response. - Decision owner: Individual run or delegated worker model. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Model adapter, effect checks, budgets, tool dispatch, snapshots and journal. - Closure path: Completed tool results and recorded decisions feed subsequent model steps. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The session or worker model selects task actions through the shipped callable loop. - Boundary reachability: The run/workflow commands construct Agent and invoke runturn; the isolated child execution calls the same goal path with its own workspace. - Evidence: Turn loop · CLI and worker execution · Model adapters - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: C - Function: Reduce conflicting changes produced by parallel workers before they reach the shared workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Two independently running child agents can produce different edits to the same relative path. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Reconcile the competing artifacts and choose the applicable work products. - Decision owner: The first-party gate reports conflicts deterministically; reconciliation is presently external to a supplied autonomous coordinator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Isolated working copies, per-path provenance, content comparison, parent drift check and exit status. - Closure path: The conflict report identifies paths and child sources and excludes disputed writes. The next apply can consume a resolved selection. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The built-in code implements a concrete sibling-conflict and feedback path. An autonomous authority to reconcile and resubmit the competing outputs still requires composition. - Boundary reachability: The standard team path defaults to isolated copies and the CLI exposes a review and guarded apply path for the resulting child artifacts. - Distinct S1 units: Parallel child model sessions with individual workspaces and journals. - Inter-S1 disturbance: Divergent changes by two children to the same destination path can overwrite each other on reintegration. - Attenuating coordination relation: Per-path conflict detection skips incompatible proposals and names contributing child sessions; identical content can proceed subject to drift checks. - Feedback into subsequent S1 behaviour: The review output exposes the conflicted paths for reconciliation and a subsequent restricted or amended apply. - Why this is S2-specific rather than generic communication / routing / sequencing / shared state / delegation: The interaction is concrete competing writes on the same shared destination, with conflict-sensitive treatment before parent work uses them. - Evidence: Team source · Parallel workflow spec · Isolation manifests · Operating mode - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: No autonomy for selecting between competing outputs is inferred from the deterministic gate. The witness is specific to isolated child integration.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Decide which outputs of the current team workflow should enter the shared workspace. - Disturbance / variety regulated: The current child portfolio can complete unevenly, conflict, or become stale against the parent workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Commit or hold each currently available child output, after reviewing the team results and affected files. - Decision owner: Workspace operator in the default inspect-then-apply path. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Team-wide output inventory, selective apply, conflict and drift enforcement, transactional return and rollback. - Closure path: The selected outputs are applied to the shared workspace after explicit action. Holding or rolling them back changes the state exposed to the next turns. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The default operator mode retains integration choice after the team finishes. The opt-in apply-all mode is deterministic under a prior choice. - Boundary reachability: The default team command reports all affected child outputs and directs an operator to first-party selective apply; the same workspace receives the committed state. - Whole-system current view: All child execution outputs for the current team, changed paths, completion receipts and merge/drift status at its declared workflow recursion. - Current-control decision scope: Select which completed work enters the live parent workspace or roll back a shared-work commit while preserving outstanding work evidence. - Evidence: Team reporting and apply · Team operating instructions · Isolated outcome explanation - Basis: structural -\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: C - Function: Independently challenge a completed-team claim against the actual workspace artifact before returning overall success. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Ordinary child reports can overstate completion or omit a failed acceptance condition. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Establish whether delivered artifacts pass the owner-selected acceptance checks independently of model prose. - Decision owner: A separately supplied acceptance command owns the proof predicate. The runtime owns exit-code enforcement and the summarizer receives the outcome. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Separate owner-configured command, direct workspace access, team acceptance journal, hard failure exit and a final model check against actual artifacts. - Closure path: A failing predicate is reported to the summarizer and forces an unsuccessful overall outcome despite a model&#39;s claim. Corrective work returns through the operator or subsequent run. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The distribution exposes a dedicated evidence and return path. The operator must compose the task-specific verifier and its independent trust boundary. - Boundary reachability: The shipped team command runs the configured acceptance command after workflow/apply and before its final evidence-informed report. - Claim being audited: A completed team result actually satisfies its task acceptance conditions. - Ordinary reporting path: The worker runs return workflow status, journals and descriptions of their proposed changes. - Complementary access path: A separately configured acceptance process inspects committed workspace evidence, with its exit status recorded independently of the model reply. - Independence boundary: The checking process and criteria are supplied from the operator boundary. Independence is limited to a correctly composed verifier. - Who acts on findings: Team runtime enforces failure at exit and provides the finding to the separate final reporting agent and operator. - Evidence: Team acceptance and exit · Ordinary worker loop · Independent observations · Team process - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: Journal replay and hash verification help reconstruction. They alone establish neither an independent task verdict nor automatic rework.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Sense external change and carry a future adaptation decision into the workspace&#39;s supported capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Provider availability, plugin options or tool changes could affect future work. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party supported loop was identified that interprets such external changes and returns an adaptation decision. - Decision owner: The operator supplies model, plugin and environment choices. Models operate within the chosen context. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider registry/config, plugin attachment, knowledge and memory checks. - Closure path: Configured capabilities affect later runs. Runtime observations inform present execution and audit. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Configuration and recall do not themselves implement external prospective adaptation. - Evidence: Configured providers · Plugins · Knowledge · CLI boundaries - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: Determine lasting identity or ultimate policy for the installation and its team operation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Tool effects and crash recovery can pose trust or intervention questions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No separate ultimate-policy decision and return path was established. - Decision owner: Code and invocation configuration set policy behavior; an operator can control approvals for current work. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Mode selection, access checks, protected paths, session approvals and historical policy replay. - Closure path: Selected enforcement is applied to effects. Crash replay asks the operator for uncertain individual effects. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The reviewed authority governs execution and local exceptions within the configured workspace. - Evidence: Policy engine · Run modes · Approval and reconciliation · CLI history audit - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/55d81f81bf5c5d193fca0fece0b4b9d4dc2ddf54/assessments/pr/prometadev-ui-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 55d81f81bf5c5d193fca0fece0b4b9d4dc2ddf54\">55d81f8 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-20T18:55:32+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mcpcpc/piglet\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Piglet</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-20\">2026-11-20</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/mcpcpc/piglet/tree/5d3179c06a7a3f1e541f5012884d98c7fddd41f1\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5d3179c06a7a3f1e541f5012884d98c7fddd41f1\">5d3179c &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Complete software tasks through iterative workspace observation and mutation. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Unknown code state, tool failures, changing user instructions and intermediate results. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the next tool action or produce the final answer based on current task evidence. - Decision owner: The model-driven agent in the session. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Runtime dispatches built-in tools, validates inputs, emits results and persists session history. - Closure path: Tool results join the model context and affect its next decision. The final response and workspace changes reach the operator. - Boundary reachability: Both supported GUI distributions enter the same session and loop path through the shipped server. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime executes requests, but removing model discretion removes the choice of task action and the response to tool results. - Evidence: src/piglet/runtime/session.py, src/piglet/runtime/session.py, src/piglet/agent/loop.py. - Basis: Explicit - Confidence: High - Caveats: Individual tools execute deterministic operations. No live provider or installed GUI was exercised here.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Coordination between concurrently usable task sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Sessions may operate in the same workspace. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No installed relation was found that negotiates conflicting session activity and feeds back to the participating units. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The manager holds session instances. Tool-call concurrency within one model response has a local file-lock map. - Closure path: No returned cross-session coordination decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A lock created within one response&#39;s tool batch attenuates intra-unit calls. Session switches and transcript branching do not regulate multiple units&#39; interference. - Evidence: src/piglet/runtime/manager.py, src/piglet/agent/loop.py, src/piglet/server/handlers.py. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: Whole-installation current control over workload commitments and resources. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Concurrent task demand and failed or interrupted execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No installation-level allocation or reprioritization loop was established. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: The GUI can inspect live sessions and the operator can prompt, steer or stop an individual session. Retry and compaction are bounded recovery. - Closure path: Local interruption and retry return to a single run. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A live-session listing plus individual controls does not establish a supported whole-installation current commitment and resource decision path. - Evidence: src/piglet/server/handlers.py, src/piglet/runtime/session.py, src/piglet/runtime/session.py. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Direct complementary audit of operational claims and corrective feedback. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A task result could overstate or conceal its actual effect. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party audit judgment path with complementary access was established. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Transcripts record tool outcomes and the GUI exposes operational events. - Closure path: No audit finding is returned to an accountable current-control owner by the standard distribution. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Transcript, export, and extension hooks expose routine execution evidence or generic integration points. - Evidence: src/piglet/runtime/export.py, src/piglet/server/hub.py, src/piglet/extensions/host.py. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Prospective adaptation of the installation to environmental change. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Shifts in provider and workspace conditions may affect future capability. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party route was found for external intelligence to generate and return a prospective capability decision. - Decision owner: None established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Provider and package configuration, compaction, retry and resource reload are shipped. - Closure path: Changes are applied when configured and recovery feeds the active session. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Current configuration or error recovery does not evaluate future external conditions and select an adaptation. - Evidence: src/piglet/runtime/resources.py, src/piglet/runtime/session.py, src/piglet/extensions/packages.py. - Basis: Structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Set the enduring mandate of the local coding agent and decide whose project-level instructions may define it. - Disturbance / variety regulated: A workspace can supply an alternative agent mandate and executable extensions under project trust. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose the global agent mandate and authorize or revoke project-level delegation to override its default preamble. - Decision owner: The local installation operator. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Global and trusted-project SYSTEM.md precedence, project trust store and UI, resource reload, and system-prompt composition. - Closure path: Operator-controlled mandate and trust are read into resources. A subsequent run carries the resulting preamble as the system prompt and applies the trusted project resource boundary. - Boundary reachability: Global and project resources are native to both local GUI distributions. The server accepts the operator&#39;s trust choice and reloads sessions. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model consumes the governing preamble. The operator chooses whether the project mandate can replace it and can revoke that delegation. - Evidence: src/piglet/server/handlers.py, src/piglet/runtime/resources.py, src/piglet/resources/contextfiles.py, src/piglet/resources/systemprompt.py, src/piglet/runtime/session.py. - Basis: Structural - Confidence: Medium - Caveats: The claim is the supported override of the enduring agent mandate in an operator-governed installation. Credential settings and task steering alone do not establish S5. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Which enduring mandate the local coding agent follows across tasks, and whether the workspace may replace the installation&#39;s default mandate. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The local operator specifies the global mandate and decides whether project-level mandate files apply. - Return-to-operation path: Trust changes reload resources. The allowed SYSTEM.md supplies the replacement preamble on subsequent runs.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/4816cc2c2ff4d8f6908ff96843b7b317ef3e3f0c/assessments/pi/piglet.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 4816cc2c2ff4d8f6908ff96843b7b317ef3e3f0c\">4816cc2 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"2\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-17T22:40:52+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DROOdotFOO/raxol-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Raxol Agent</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>2</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-17\">2026-11-17</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/DROOdotFOO/raxol-agent/tree/e5b500988139674f39d957309b1db34988edc08a\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision e5b500988139674f39d957309b1db34988edc08a\">e5b5009 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Execute model-guided tasks through a first-party Elixir ReAct agent with tool feedback and typed outputs. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Open prompts, changing tool response data, unexpected tool failures and model-selected next actions. - Decisive decision or feedback right: In Raxol.Agent.Stream.react or Strategy.ReAct the model selects tool calls, adapts to tool results and stops or continues. - Decision owner: The configured model-backed first-party stream/strategy operating an agent in the supported session or scheduler mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: OTP session, provider adapters, tool converter, command hooks, authorization, journals and optional scheduler. - Closure path: Prompt/observations → model-selected tool → guarded executor result → next model action/final response. - Boundary reachability: Shipped Stream.react, packaged strategy and optional Console scheduler fire are first-party runnable agent paths, not development-only examples. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model owns choice of task action; fixed scheduler/OTP services are supporting enforcement and recovery. - Evidence: ReAct loop; strategy; scheduled runner - Basis: structural -\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: No first-party S2-specific agent interaction/regulation loop established at the selected managed-agent fleet boundary. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Agent processes could compete for files, objectives or communications; the shipped supervisor focuses on crash recovery and message routing. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No first-party arbitration of a concrete inter-agent conflicting commitment was found beyond identity and lifecycle checks. - Decision owner: Not established as an S2 decision maker. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Agent.Team supervisor restforone, Registry and Comm routes, per-session ownership and spawn-id guard. - Closure path: An OTP restart or message delivery occurs, but it does not choose among competing S1 commitments or return a conflict-resolution intervention. - Boundary reachability: The team/communication primitives ship, but generic supervision is not automatically S2. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A user-written coordinator callback could negotiate, but no standard coordinator-agent rule observing conflict and changing teammate behavior was identified. - Evidence: team supervision; team comm; owned session restrictions - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: C - Function: Provide a fleet-scoped current agent roster, status inspection, start/stop and resumed work controls for an MCP-managed ownership cohort. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Managed agents may be absent, running, stalled or no longer needed; current capacity and task commitments need supervisory intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect and choose which managed agent to spawn, query or stop and change the active managed-agent population. - Decision owner: MCP caller/host or explicitly assembled supervisory model, not a first-party agent supervisor in the default Raxol process. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: MCP agentlist/status/ask/spawn/stop, session owner checks, journal retention, OTP supervisor and optional scheduler. - Closure path: Agent roster/status → external supervisor&#39;s decision to spawn/stop/ask → owned session starts/stops or receives new task; later MCP status reflects outcome. - Boundary reachability: Only when built-in MCP tools are enabled in a supported registered mode; they restrict visibility/stop to this server&#39;s owned agents, excluding foreign/team members. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The first-party S3-specific current-state/action path exists, but a model with authority over the fleet and closed supervisory discretion must be composed by the embedding operator/host; the runtime cannot choose it. - Whole-system current view: agentlist and status for the MCP-owned cohort; a different owner or Team child is not part of this current-control claim. - Current-control decision scope: Start/stop/ask and monitor current operational agents within server ownership, not arbitrary host-wide scheduling or policies. - Evidence: bounded fleet controls; owned session lifecycle; MCP mode - Basis: structural - Confidence: medium - Caveats: C is narrow and boundary-relative. A generic agentstatus API outside an operational cohort would not qualify; no autonomous S3=A mode or parent-specific P mode is claimed.\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: No first-party independent operational-claim audit loop demonstrated in the installed managed agent runtime. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Incorrect model claims and incomplete action outcomes might require complementary primary evidence. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No shipped autonomous auditor with separate evidence access and returned judgement of an S1 result established. - Decision owner: Not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Session journal and activity records, authorized tool gates and result streams; developers can write a CodeReviewAgent example. - Closure path: Messages/journal return work information, but no separate verifier sees different operational evidence and returns an authoritative correction. - Boundary reachability: team sample and logging are available, not a first-party completed S3 audit mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A :reviewer label in example team is a user-supplied module and not evidence of independent agent audit or S3 feedback. - Evidence: custom reviewer team; generic supervisor; communication and model view - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-c\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: C - Function: Expose procedural skill creation/modification for adaptation to future task varieties, without a fully installed autonomous after-turn improvement owner. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New tasks may expose missing procedures and outdated skill content needed in later scheduled or interactive runs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An embedding agent/operator can create/patch first-party managed SKILL.md procedures through skillmanage, to be retrieved/injected into later work. - Decision owner: The calling model or human in a developer-composed mode; standard runtime does not independently decide when experiences warrant future skill revision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Skills.Store, skillmanage, read-only external skill dirs, versioned SKILL.md disk persistence, scheduler&#39;s skill-block injection. - Closure path: New skill gap and deliberately invoked skillmanage → durable managed skill → later list/view or scheduled-run injection. The missing detection/independent prospective judgement must be composed. - Boundary reachability: First-party actions appear when an agent supplies skillsprovider; documented callback defaults nil and must be configured. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: A function-specific future-capability constructor exists, but removed SelfImprove/Curator and Turn recorder mean no ordinary completed agent-owned feedback cycle; C not A. - External distinction: A newly encountered task requirement or outside skill source compared with currently indexed procedural assets. - Future / prospective distinction: Saved SKILL.md is durable across runs and can alter later prompts/available routines, not simply a one-turn memory buffer. - Adaptation option generated: A created or edited procedural skill with typed metadata and body in managed skills root. - Path back into current capability / S3: Skills.Store subsequently supplies the changed skill to agent tools or scheduler Fire system-prompt composition. - Evidence: skill construction; persistent reusable skills; future run injection; explicitly narrowed ADR - Basis: structural -\">C</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: No native ultimate-identity/policy tension and authoritative ratification with returned rule revision established. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Conflicting standing mission or constitutional limits are different from tool-call approval, scope and spend safety. - Decisive decision or feedback right: No in-boundary ultimate-policy adjudication path evidenced. - Decision owner: External operator supplies permissions and manifests; enforcement remains deterministic. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Authorization.Engine ALLOW/ASK/DENY, root/session remembered approval, MCP authorizer, allowlisted applications and spending constraints. - Closure path: A tool ASK can be approved and remembered at action/session scope; no identity-level decision changes subsequent organizational purpose. - Boundary reachability: Policy modules are shipped but have no S5 deliberation-and-return mode. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Approval of a tool call and failure-closed limits do not constitute ultimate-policy ownership or future constitutional settlement. - Evidence: tool approval engine; bounded actor ownership; current narrowed architecture - Basis: structural -\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/2d6790e0c7329909c5b5013b6fc4a39732de6d8f/assessments/ra/raxol-agent.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 2d6790e0c7329909c5b5013b6fc4a39732de6d8f\">2d6790e &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-19T16:51:36+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cppalliance/runvil-harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">runvil-harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-19\">2026-11-19</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/cppalliance/runvil-harness/tree/ee199e3ae04366e02c61488c096f2981c057ff0b\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision ee199e3ae04366e02c61488c096f2981c057ff0b\">ee199e3 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: execute an authored operational prompt using model-selected tool actions and returned observations. - Disturbance / variety regulated: unknown external information and tool outcomes in a prompt&#39;s bounded task. - Decisive decision or feedback right: choose offered tools/arguments and whether to continue or return an answer. - Decision owner: the model bound to the active prompt role. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Engine/Lua state machine, effect driver, inference broker, Pack registry, tool limits and recorder. - Closure path: models.loop returns model tool requests to the first-party executor and appends observations for later rounds; final content becomes the run result. - Boundary reachability: the library exposes Harness::run and models.loop; the supplied researchperson prompt wires web tools and iterative model work. A Host supplies documented broker/timer/configuration bindings, rather than implementing the agent loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: the engine&#39;s deterministic stepping is support: replacing the model with no decision-maker would not select the research actions. The offline greeting example alone is not the positive witness. - Evidence: shipped research prompt, models.loop adapter, models.loop state machine, effect loop, gateway broker - Basis: structural. - Confidence: high. - Caveats: library deployment requires a Host, inference service and installed Packs. Source inspection does not assert a separately shipped end-user UI or a live provider test.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: no supplied function-specific regulation of interference between distinct operational cells. - Disturbance / variety regulated: parallel chains, effects and shared VFS operations could contend, but the supplied mechanisms regulate execution mechanics. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no installed inter-S1 disturbance-response decision/feedback path established. - Decision owner: developer-authored scheduling and host policy; no established S2 actor. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: oldest-first tool-call cap, synchronous VFS operations, per-execution stores and generic chains. - Closure path: effects resume the requesting chain; this is ordinary execution correlation, not a supplied inter-unit conflict-settlement loop. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: multiple futures/chains and ordered file operations alone do not establish S2 or justify C. - Evidence: effect loop, runtime boundary, RunSetup, shipped research prompt - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Bounded source-based absence; custom Host/prompt organizations and external Runvil products are not credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: — - Function: no material whole-system resource/commitment regulation path supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: per-run limits and external interruption, rather than current whole-system bargaining or intervention. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no first-party current-control actor selects portfolio priorities or resource reallocations from a whole-system view. - Decision owner: Host author/operator sets limits and sends messages; that extensibility alone is not a closed parent S3 mode. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: RunLimits, maxtoolcallsinflight, RunControl send/stop/cancel and recorded events. - Closure path: a stop/cancel is enforced in one run; no packaged observer→current-control judgment→returned whole-system intervention is supplied. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a kill/steer API and performance records are support, not proof of S3 ownership. - Evidence: RunSetup, Harness and RunControl, effect loop, recorder interface - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Bounded source-based absence; custom Host/prompt organizations and external Runvil products are not credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: no independent complementary audit path of the runtime&#39;s operational claims supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: run fidelity is observable through records and deterministic tests, without a packaged independent claim challenge. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no audit-specific evidence judgment and corrective return. - Decision owner: not established. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: event/effect/answer recording, typed receipts, deterministic replayability and tests. - Closure path: records report the same production execution; no distinct auditor feeds findings into subsequent control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: recording every effect and testing the engine do not create complementary S3 judgment. - Evidence: recorder interface, effect loop, runtime boundary - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Bounded source-based absence; custom Host/prompt organizations and external Runvil products are not credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: no supplied external/prospective adaptation loop for the harness&#39;s current capability. - Disturbance / variety regulated: researchperson senses the external web to answer its task; this is the product&#39;s S1 outcome. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no prospective harness-adaptation option judgment with return into present capability. - Decision owner: prompt/Host author selects models and Packs; model chooses operational search, not an established S4 decision. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: web tools, model-role requirements, models.use, plugin loading and context compaction. - Closure path: research facts return as a summary; static model bindings and compaction do not feed prospective capability options into current control. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: external sensing alone, or changing the model by authored Lua, does not establish S4. - Evidence: shipped research prompt, role selection example, RunSetup, models.loop adapter - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Bounded source-based absence; custom Host/prompt organizations and external Runvil products are not credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: — - Function: no runtime identity/ultimate-policy closure path supplied. - Disturbance / variety regulated: tool/file permissions and scope constraints regulate ordinary execution. - Decisive decision or feedback right: no identity/policy escalation to legitimate ultimate authority with a returned governing resolution. - Decision owner: Host/developer configuration supplies static scope and VFS policies; no established S5 owner. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: frozen tool offers, Policy trait, workspace access, hasuser inbox and cancellation. - Closure path: allow/deny, user messages and run stops affect task execution; no identity/ultimate-policy transaction closes. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: a policy interface and user input are not themselves S5. - Evidence: VFS Policy, public Harness/Host API, RunSetup, Harness and RunControl - Basis: structural. - Confidence: medium-high. - Caveats: Bounded source-based absence; custom Host/prompt organizations and external Runvil products are not credited.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/e053e838e2b813f4288460bb15e28c754314fc1a/assessments/ru/runvil-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit e053e838e2b813f4288460bb15e28c754314fc1a\">e053e83 &#8599;</a></td></tr>","<tr data-a=\"1\" data-c=\"0\" data-assessment-at=\"2026-11-16T14:40:26+03:00\"><th scope=\"row\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Antoine-Thomas/UH-Universal-Harness\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UH — Universal Harness</a><small>2026</small></th><td class=\"counts-cell\" data-sort-value=\"1\"><div class=\"vhi-counts\"><span class=\"vhi-count count-a\">A <b>1</b></span><span class=\"vhi-count count-c\">C <b>0</b></span></div></td><td class=\"review-date\"><time datetime=\"2026-11-16\">2026-11-16</time></td><td class=\"review-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/Antoine-Thomas/UH-Universal-Harness/tree/5b63113bc6096779500bb1c99e624919bcf48fc0\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open pinned source revision 5b63113bc6096779500bb1c99e624919bcf48fc0\">5b63113 &#8599;</a></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-a\" title=\"S1 / operation: - State: A - Function: Complete user-directed work through a tool-using session. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Different tasks, tool results, failed tool calls and incomplete intermediate outputs. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Select the next admitted tool call and its arguments, revise the task response using results, or finish the turn. - Decision owner: The session&#39;s model-backed agent in the first-party turn loop. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Tool definitions, result/error transport, context preparation, iteration limit and optional remote-tool approval. - Closure path: The response proposes a tool call, the tool result enters history and the next model iteration acts on it or returns the final answer; the session emits and records the result. - Boundary reachability: CLI and cockpit invoke ChatSession, which directly binds the provider and admitted toolsets to runturn. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The runtime executes bounded decisions while the model chooses how to proceed within them. The operator can stop a turn and gate remote tools. - Evidence: uh/agent.py, uh/console.py, uh/console.py, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md. - Basis: Implemented turn loop and supported entry-point wiring. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: Scope is task execution within a session.\">A</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S2 / coordination: - State: — - Function: Attenuate interference between concurrent operational sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Potential contention for workspace, processing and provider capacity. - Decisive decision or feedback right: An inter-session coordination outcome that changes subsequent behaviour of distinct sessions. - Decision owner: No first-party witness in the reviewed modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Per-session histories, provider ordering/cooldown and host-idle scheduling for Dream. - Closure path: No inter-session feedback relation reconstructed. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Tool sequencing operates inside one turn. Provider fallback handles inference availability for calls. Dream&#39;s daemon observes host idle state and runs a bounded background pass. - Evidence: uh/agent.py, uh/router/provider.py, uh/dreamdaemon.py, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md. - Basis: Source inspection of session execution, fallback and Dream scheduling. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: Host scheduling and an operator&#39;s manual coordination can still affect contention.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S3 / regulation: - State: P - Function: Control the current installation&#39;s default inference allocation across operational sessions. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Changes in installed models, observed resource pressure, session usage and availability of inference backends. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Choose and persist the installation&#39;s default provider/model pair and apply it to subsequent default operation. - Decision owner: The authenticated local operator, who can consult the cockpit&#39;s installation-level resource and usage view. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Aggregate session decisions and host resource telemetry, model/provider compatibility checks, authenticated model switch, application-state and user-config writes. - Closure path: The cockpit returns aggregate usage and host availability to the operator; the operator selects the default pair, which the web route validates, applies to app state and stores for subsequent launch. Existing explicit session overrides remain scoped to their session. - Boundary reachability: The telemetry and no-session model switch routes are shipped together in the web operating mode. The persist-and-use path is available inside the installed package. - Whole-system current view: Session, cost and Dream records are aggregated with host CPU, RAM and VRAM for the installation. - Current-control decision scope: Default inference allocation across the installation. Per-call retry, cooldown and max-iteration enforcement are runtime support. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The operator reads the overview and selects the installation default. Provider fallback follows a configured strategy within inference requests. - Evidence: uh/web/app.py, uh/telemetry.py, uh/web/app.py, docs/ARCHITECTURE.md. - Basis: Shipped web telemetry and installation-default model switch. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: This supports parent current resource control at installation scope. It does not establish broader control over user task commitments.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S3* / direct audit: - State: — - Function: Independently check material operational claims made through ordinary session reporting. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Divergence between reported outcomes and what tools or sessions accomplished. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Inspect primary operational reality through a complementary channel, form an audit judgment and return it to a current-control owner. - Decision owner: No first-party witness in the reviewed modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Turn journal, session decisions, read-only Dream inventory, connector probes and repair checks. - Closure path: The reviewed surfaces close reporting, connector setup or routine repair validation. No separate operational outcome audit loop was established. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Dream checks notebook and tooling samples for knowledge defects and feasible workflow ideas. It has no observed mandate to verify session claims from independent execution evidence. - Evidence: uh/web/app.py, uh/web/app.py, uh/dreampass.py, uh/dreampass.py, uh/fixes.py. - Basis: Source inspection of both ordinary reporting and candidate complementary paths. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: Direct MCP inventory checks validate exposed tooling; the reviewed target is independent audit of session operation.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-none\" title=\"S4 / adaptation: - State: — - Function: Prospectively adapt installation capability to environmental change. - Disturbance / variety regulated: New tooling, changing knowledge sources and possible future workflows. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Decide which prospective option to adopt and return that decision into current capability. - Decision owner: Dream generates proposals. No owner-to-capability transition from the prospective proposals was evidenced in the reviewed modes. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Optional knowledge and MCP inventory, provider-generated ideas, persisted reports and operator plan export. - Closure path: The supported Dream flow reaches a report or plan file; neither its fulfill route nor its plan writer applies the proposal. The separate fix flow addresses operator-described breakage and has no Dream-selection input. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: The model contributes prospective ideas and the operator can export them. The installed feedback path ends at delivery of the plan. - Evidence: uh/dreampass.py, uh/dreampass.py, uh/dreamstore.py, uh/web/app.py, uh/web/app.py. - Basis: Trace from environment inventory to argument validation, output and operator action. - Confidence: Medium. - Caveats: An operator can manually implement an idea outside this workflow; the reviewed boundary is the shipped return path.\">—</abbr></td><td class=\"state-cell\"><abbr tabindex=\"0\" class=\"vhi-state state-p\" title=\"S5 / identity: - State: P - Function: Determine the installation&#39;s trust boundary for the integrated free-provider mode. - Disturbance / variety regulated: Failure or unavailability of local inference creates a choice between remaining local and admitting remote inference. - Decisive decision or feedback right: Permit or prohibit the integrated free router&#39;s remote use of session data and persist that deployment policy. - Decision owner: The installation owner through operator configuration, local-only override and first-use consent. - Supporting / enforcement mechanisms: Merged config, environment and local-only precedence, persisted consent, router refusal before use. - Closure path: The owner sets the remote policy and supplies any required consent; subsequent integrated-router calls enforce it. Local-only overrides or denial keep that route closed. - Boundary reachability: The shipped router invokes the policy code, reads the installation configuration and enforces it at runtime. - Identity / ultimate-policy issue: Which data may cross the local-first deployment boundary in the integrated-router mode. - Ultimate authority in each claimed mode: The installation owner controls the configuration, startup flags and consent. The router and session agent operate within that choice. - Return-to-operation path: Stored config/consent or an explicit local-only override is read on later use and enforces the decision. - Why this is / is not agent-owned: Provider fallback is bounded by the operator&#39;s durable trust policy and cannot grant itself remote authority. - Evidence: uh/router/policy.py, uh/router/provider.py, uh/config.py, README.md. - Basis: Shipped policy configuration and pre-call enforcement. - Confidence: High. - Caveats: The witness concerns the integrated free router. Explicitly configured remote providers and MCP servers have their own paths.\">P</abbr></td><td class=\"assessment-ref\"><a href=\"https://github.com/opensiro/vsm-harness-index/blob/421dbbf2bb7d2d739cd32bbddf2a3d22223c4d12/assessments/uh/uh-universal-harness.md\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" title=\"Open assessment at commit 421dbbf2bb7d2d739cd32bbddf2a3d22223c4d12\">421dbbf &#8599;</a></td></tr>"];
